#!/usr/bin/env bash
# promote-released-cards — move a release's tracking cards to a "released" stage.
#
# Generic across repos (sibling to release-pr-body). Board / stage come from
# `.release-pr.json` (`.promote` block); the API BASE comes from $KANBAN_API_BASE, falling
# back to `.promote.api_base`; the API token comes from $KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN and is only
# ever sent to a base that passed the $KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST guard (see ENVIRONMENT below).
# A card is "shipped in this release" if its
# `payload.dl_number` matches a shipped DL token, its `payload.pr_number` matches a
# shipped PR number, or its own `id` is in an explicitly-passed `--cards` set — so
# cards tracked only by PR (no DL) are promoted too — AND, for the two REF fields, its
# by-ref `source` qualifies against `.promote.source` (see REPO QUALIFICATION below).
# Idempotent (skips a card already at target), best-effort per card.
# A card it moves has every seat owner tag (`owner:*`) removed by a SEPARATE tag write after the
# move (the rule is agent-board-toolkit README.md § The seat owner tag); that write's outcome is
# printed — from a read-back of the card's tags, not from the PATCH's status (card#9938) — and
# never changes the move's report, the counts, or the exit code.
# With the opt-in `--shipped-stages IDS` guard, a matched card NOT currently in a
# Shipped-class source stage is skipped, not moved (promotion never resurrects a
# declined/backlog card carrying a stale/recycled stamp); see the Usage note below.
# A matched card carrying the `program` tag is WITHHELD — always, no flag — because such a card
# is a PARENT naming several legs and no one release may take it terminal; see THE PROGRAM-PARENT
# WITHHOLD below.
#
# THE PROGRAM-PARENT WITHHOLD — unconditional, non-fatal, counted (toolkit card#10068).
#   A card carrying the `program` tag names SEVERAL LEGS rather than one deliverable. A release
#   ships one of them, the card's `dl_number`/`pr_number` matches, and this mover then puts the
#   PARENT in the terminal stage — the one nobody re-reads — while its other legs are unbuilt and
#   stop being offered by any queue. The work is still written on the card; the card just reads
#   DONE. The live instance (kanban card#9996, seven legs, one built) was held out of this by a
#   hand-set `block_reason` and a source-stage pin, i.e. by COLUMN, not by any predicate.
#
#   WHY THE PREDICATE IS THE TAG AND NOT A PARENT/CHILD RELATION. Measured on the boards this
#   fleet runs, and stated as the comparison rather than as a pair of figures that would rot —
#   re-derive it by asking each board for the tag, beside a control tag known to be in use, so an
#   empty answer is a FILTER result and not an empty corpus: the `program` tag SELECTS the parents
#   it is put on; `parent_id` — which now exists in the board schema — selects NOTHING, because
#   nothing backfills it, and on the row being MOVED it marks that row as a CHILD, so a predicate
#   keyed on it would refuse exactly the legs
#   that should move and permit the parent it exists to hold back. The structurally right
#   predicate is the inverse, *has children*, and the board's task resource does not project it,
#   so no external writer can read it at all yet. Revisit when it does, not before.
#
#   WHY WITHHOLD AND NOT REFUSE, which is the whole shape of this guard and not a soft option.
#   This tool is VENDORED, SHA-PINNED, into other repos' release workflows, so a hard failure here
#   becomes THEIR broken release for a card that is not theirs; and on an install with no bridge
#   this is the ONLY writer of the terminal stage, which is exactly where the predicate most needs
#   to exist rather than to be load-bearing on an exit code. A withheld card is left where it was
#   and is recoverable by hand on any later day; a terminal one is not. So: the card is named on
#   stderr with the `⊘` the source-stage guard already uses, counted in its own summary segment,
#   and the run continues. A WITHHELD CARD NEVER CHANGES THE RUN'S EXIT CODE: THE RUN EXITS AS IF
#   THE MOVE HAD LANDED. That follows from WHERE the withhold sits — it is the LAST check before the
#   write, after already-released, `--shipped-stages` and `--require-complete` — so a parent any
#   of those stops is reported, counted and exited on exactly as it was before the withhold existed
#   (`already released`, `stage-guarded`, `completeness-refused`), and the tag is consulted only
#   for a card that would otherwise MOVE. For that card its withheld count stands in for the
#   `moved` it would have counted, in the two arms that ask whether a run promoted anything (EXIT
#   CODES: the refused-move route of 1 and the ref-completeness die of 2). ⚠ THE ONE CASE IT
#   CANNOT MATCH: a parent whose PATCH the pre-withhold tool would have sent and seen FAIL
#   (refused, NOT APPLIED, unread). Knowing that takes the write this guard exists not to make, so
#   the run is decided as if the move had landed — which can only LOWER an rc, never raise it.
#
#   ⛔ THE SPELLING IS AN EXACT, CASE-SENSITIVE, WHITESPACE-SENSITIVE MATCH ON THE TAG `program`,
#   which is written here as the literal an operator reading `--help` needs (this block IS the
#   published contract for a copy vendored standalone with no docs beside it) rather than as a
#   pointer to the constant below — and the two are held equal by a cell in
#   `tests/promote-program-withhold-selftest.sh`, so this copy cannot drift from the predicate. And
#   that is not this file's choice to make: the board stores tags verbatim (it normalizes neither
#   case nor whitespace) and the bridge's own `ProgramCardGuard` — the other writer that consults
#   this tag — matches exactly. `Program` and `program ` are a DIFFERENT tag to that writer, so
#   admitting them here would make the two writers disagree about which cards are parents, which
#   is worse than either rule alone. A second spelling would be a second rule.
#
#   ⚠ WHAT THIS CANNOT SEE, so `0 program-withheld` is not read as "no parent was promoted": a
#   parent nobody TAGGED is invisible to this, exactly as it is to the bridge's guard — the tag is
#   an operator convention with no enforcement at mint time on either side. And a row whose `tags`
#   this run could not read as a list answers "not a parent" and is PROMOTED; that direction is
#   deliberate and is the bridge guard's too (the alternative withholds a whole release on one
#   malformed response), but it means an unreadable `tags` is silent here.
#
# Shipped refs come from an EXPLICIT set (`--dls "DL-1,DL-2"` and/or `--cards "12,34"`),
# else from `git log <base>..<head>`:
#   - DL tokens via `ref_token_regex` (default DL-[0-9]+), matched on the numeric part
#     against a card's `payload.dl_number`;
#   - PR numbers from each commit's trailing `(#NNN)` (the squash-merge marker GitHub appends),
#     matched against `payload.pr_number`;
#   - card IDs from `--cards` only, matched against the card's own `id`.
#
# WHY CARD IDS ARE EXPLICIT-ONLY AND NEVER DERIVED FROM COMMIT SUBJECTS. A `card#NNNN`
# token in a subject is not reliably a claim that the commit SHIPS that card — descriptive
# citations ("supersedes card#1234", "sibling of card#5678") are common and are already
# known to hijack correlation elsewhere. For a DL token that misfire is bounded by the
# card also needing a matching `dl_number` stamp; for an id it is not — the id IS the
# match, so a passing mention would relocate an unrelated card. Deriving ids from subjects
# is therefore left to the caller, and the one caller that does it (release-pr-body's
# read-only coverage report) passes them with --dry-run, where the worst outcome is a
# coverage line that reads "covered". Do not "complete" this by adding a
# `card_token_regex` read to the derive path below.
#
# REPO QUALIFICATION — `.promote.source`, REQUIRED, no default (card#8421).
#   `dl_number` and `pr_number` are REPO-SCOPED counters. On a board that tracks SEVERAL
#   repos they COLLIDE, and a bare-number correlation then promotes ANOTHER repo's cards.
#   MEASURED by a reporting peer on a board carrying three repos: one release's 15 shipped
#   PRs matched 24 cards across all three lanes — including cards already sitting at a
#   released stage from a DIFFERENT repo's own version line. So this mover REFUSES to run
#   until it is told which arity of board it is pointed at. There is no safe default, for
#   the same reason $KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST has none: a vendored tool cannot assume the
#   operator's topology, and the unsafe assumption here is a WRITE into a terminal stage.
#     `.promote.source`: "<owner>/<repo>"  — the repo this release ships. Every REF-matched
#        card is qualified against it by the card's own by-ref `source`; a card belonging to
#        a different repo is NOT promoted, and is named. See `derive_source` in the
#        correlation below for how a card's source is read.
#     `.promote.source`: "*"               — an explicit DECLARATION that this board tracks
#        exactly ONE repo, so a bare `dl_number`/`pr_number` is unambiguous on it.
#        Byte-identical to the pre-card#8421 behavior, summary line included. `*` is a
#        DECLARATION, not a wildcard match: use it only when the board really is single-repo.
#   `--source <owner/repo>|*` overrides the config for one run (a dry-run pointed elsewhere).
#
#   AND THE DECLARATION IS CHECKED AGAINST THE REPO IT IS MADE IN (card#8538). Whichever of
#   the two channels wins, a resolved source other than `*` must equal $GITHUB_REPOSITORY
#   when that variable is set — GitHub sets it to the repository the workflow runs in, which
#   on the promote runner IS the repo being released. A typo'd or copy-pasted slug otherwise
#   passes every shape check and correlates against nothing: 0 promoted, rc 0, silent. Off a
#   runner ($GITHUB_REPOSITORY unset) there is no repo identity to check and the leg does not
#   fire; `*` is a declaration rather than a repo name and does not fire either.
#
#   ⚠ A card with NO derivable by-ref source cannot be attributed under a qualified run: it is
#   NOT promoted, and it is named individually and counted — never dropped silently. That
#   inverts the reconciler's "a null source matches every repo" rule ON PURPOSE, and the
#   inversion is an asymmetry rather than a disagreement: that rule governs a DEFER, whose
#   failure mode is to do nothing, whereas this is a WRITE into a terminal stage — there,
#   "matches every repo" means EVERY repo's release promotes that one unstamped card. Fix the
#   card, not the guard: stamp a repo-qualified `payload.pr_url`
#   (`kbcard patch --pr-url https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/<N>`) and it correlates.
#   Its shipped ref is NOT reported as "no card" — that would send `bin/release-pr-body`'s
#   card-coverage report to tell the author to CREATE a card that is already on the board.
#   It gets its own report line, and that section renders it as "card exists but carries no
#   by-ref source — stamp it". See THE NO-CARD REPORT, below the correlation.
#
#   ⛔ `--cards` IS EXEMPT, and the exemption is this rule's own logic, not a hole in it.
#   Qualification exists because a dl/pr NUMBER is repo-scoped; a card `id` is not — it names
#   exactly one card on exactly one board, so there is no collision for a guard to break.
#   Refusing an id the caller named EXPLICITLY would refuse an unambiguous instruction, and it
#   would make `bin/release-pr-body`'s coverage line for any other-repo card on a shared board
#   read "no card". An id-matched card whose source is not this repo IS promoted, and is NAMED
#   on stderr, so the exemption is legible in the log instead of silent.
#
# Robustness (these states are silent-but-wrong, so they're made loud):
#   - 0 visible cards on page 1 => FAIL (token's user not a board member / wrong instance).
#   - Reads the WHOLE board by paging (`page=N`), so a board >200 cards is not truncated.
#   - A 2xx page carrying no card ARRAY => FAIL, on ANY page (card#6630): `.data // []` turned
#     an unreadable later page into `[]`, which is a short page, which ENDED the scan — so the
#     tool promoted from a board it had only partly read, at rc 0.
#   - A server SHORT READ (pages deliver fewer rows than the board's own meta.total) => FAIL:
#     promoting from an incomplete scan would silently leave a shipped card un-promoted.
#   - An EXPLICIT --base/--head naming a ref this repo does not have => FAIL (rc 2, naming the
#     flag and the value). `git log` runs under `2>/dev/null || true`, which turned git's rc-128
#     "bad object" into an EMPTY subject list — so the run printed "no shipped refs in range —
#     nothing to do" and exited 0: a promotion that promoted nothing and reported success, with
#     the release's cards left un-promoted (card#7535). A DEFAULTED leg is not affected; its own
#     refusals below already cover it.
#   - A shipped ref that matched NO card is reported at the end (a card may be unstamped). A ref
#     whose ONLY match was a card with no derivable source is reported SEPARATELY, on its own
#     line: that card exists and carries the ref, so "create a card" is the wrong remedy for it
#     and "stamp this card" is the right one (see THE NO-CARD REPORT below).
#   - `.promote.source` absent => FAIL before any read (the repo-scoped ref collision above).
#   - `.promote.source` present but naming a repo other than $GITHUB_REPOSITORY (when set)
#     => FAIL before any read: the declaration is false of the repo it is made in.
#   - A ref-matched card belonging to ANOTHER repo, or to NO derivable repo, is named
#     individually and counted — never silently promoted, and never silently dropped.
#   - A REFUSED card move, and a refused board read, name the HTTP STATUS and carry a bounded,
#     secret-masked excerpt of the SERVER'S OWN body (card#9301). `api()` used to be
#     `curl -fsS`, and `-f` DISCARDS the HTTP error body — so a 401 (token expired), a 403 (the
#     role lacks `task.update`), a 409 (the card moved under us) and a 422 (wrong stage id for
#     this board) all reached the operator as the one sentence `✗ <ref> (#<id>): move failed
#     (left in place)`, with the released cards left in the wrong column and the next action a
#     guess. What did NOT change is which answers count as success: `-f` failed at 400 and
#     above, and that boundary is reproduced exactly, 3xx included (see api() below).
#     ⛔ A REFUSAL AND A TRANSPORT FAILURE ARE REPORTED AS DIFFERENT CLAIMS, because only one of
#     them knows where the card ended up: a completed non-2xx reads `move failed (left in
#     place)`, while a request that never completed reads `move NOT CONFIRMED` — the connection
#     can die AFTER the server applied the PATCH, so on that branch the card may already have
#     moved and the tool says so instead of asserting otherwise.
#   - A card move is reported from a READ-BACK, never from the 2xx (card#9938). The PATCH's
#     status is the server saying it ACCEPTED the request; only a read says what the board now
#     holds, and the two came apart on this fleet on 2026-05-22 — 28 of 28 cards PATCHed, every
#     call 2xx, `updated_at` bumped on every card, `workflow_stage_id` unchanged on every card,
#     `✓ … moved` printed 28 times, rc 0. So after a successful stage PATCH the card is re-read
#     and `workflow_stage_id` is RULED on, and that ruling is what the line and the counts say:
#       moved        the read agrees with what was asked → `✓ … moved … (read back: …)`.
#       NOT APPLIED  the read is a MEASUREMENT and it disagrees → `✗ … move NOT APPLIED`,
#                    counted on its own. The 2026-05-22 state, said out loud — and the release
#                    that met it carried 28 cards, so an rc conditioned on `moved == 0` is rc 0
#                    the moment ONE of them lands: 27 promoted and one measured un-applied is
#                    the shape this outcome exists to red, not a corner of it.
#       UNVERIFIED   the read is not a measurement (it did not complete, was refused, or carried
#                    no readable stage) → `⚠ … move UNVERIFIED`, counted separately. Nothing
#                    says the card moved, nothing says it did not. This is card#8556's third
#                    outcome, adopted rather than re-spelled: the rc, the word and the ladder
#                    are `bin/kbcard`'s.
#     The rc each of the three carries — and what each is CONDITIONAL on — is stated once, in
#     EXIT CODES below, and deliberately not repeated here.
#     The owner-tag clear is read back the same way; its outcome still changes no count and no
#     exit code (see the paragraph above). The ONE extra request per moved card is the clear's
#     own read-back — the FIRST read is shared, not added: the clear already re-read the card.
#   - Nothing NEWLY promoted (and no program parent withheld) despite a non-empty shipped-ref
#     set => the exit is decided
#     by the ROOT CAUSE: a MERGE-commit release tip (or an explicit --dls/--cards set) means
#     ref-derivation was COMPLETE, so the refs are genuinely cardless => WARN (exit 0); a
#     non-merge tip means
#     a squash/rebase merge may have dropped the per-PR refs (the v0.32.0 silent-green class)
#     => FAIL (exit 2). This converges two divergent prior behaviors: the bridge copy
#     blanket-failed any 0-promoted run (DL-165), the toolkit/kanban copies exited 0
#     silently — neither distinguished the cause.
#   - Assumes it fires on a RELEASE merge to a protected main (push==release-merge). A plain
#     non-merge commit pushed directly to main whose range carries cardless DLs would fail
#     loud by the same rule — intended, given the assumption.
#
# EXIT CODES — THE ONE STATEMENT OF THIS TOOL'S LADDER (card#9938).
#   ⛔ EVERY OTHER SURFACE POINTS HERE INSTEAD OF RESTATING IT. The set of pointers is not listed
#   — a list is a count of whatever it counted, and it goes stale the day a surface is added or
#   renamed. RE-DERIVE it instead, from the repo root:
#       command grep -rn 'EXIT CODES' README.md docs/ promote/ bin/promote-released-cards
#   Round 2 of card#9938 changed the ladder and then made "the three published statements" true
#   one by one; a fourth, `README.md`'s, was nobody's and stayed pre-round-2 — the defect was not
#   that a sentence was missed, it was that NOBODY DERIVED THE SET. Restating the ladder in an
#   N+1th place is how that happens again. Change it HERE, and leave the other surfaces pointing.
#   `promote-released-cards --help` prints this block, so the contract ships inside the vendored
#   copy and a consumer needs neither this repo nor its docs to read it.
#   ⚠ WHAT "THE LADDER" MEANS HERE, because the scope is the whole claim: what each rc MEANS and
#   what each is CONDITIONAL on. A `docs/CHANGELOG.md` entry still NAMES the codes a release moved
#   — that is a record of a delta at a moment, not a live contract, and it is not a copy of this.
#
#   0  the run promoted what it says it promoted: no card was measured in another stage, and no
#      write went unread. ⚠ NOT "every matched card is released" — a stage-guarded skip, a
#      program-parent withhold, a no-card or other-repo ref, and the refusal case below all live
#      at 0. The program withhold is at 0 BY DESIGN and not by omission: see THE PROGRAM-PARENT
#      WITHHOLD above for why this vendored tool reports that card instead of failing its
#      consumer's release over it: the withhold is the last check before the write, so the run
#      exits as if the parent's move had landed. ⛔ A GATE THAT MUST CATCH ONE READS THE
#      `program-withheld` SEGMENT OF THE SUMMARY LINE, NOT THE RC.
#   1  a KNOWN failure. TWO ROUTES REACH IT AND THEY ARE NOT CONDITIONAL ON THE SAME THING —
#      the difference is the whole of what round 2 changed, so it is stated rather than implied:
#        · a MEASURED un-applied move (`✗ … move NOT APPLIED`) — UNCONDITIONAL. Whatever else
#          the run moved, skipped or could not read: the board was read and it disagrees with
#          the write, so the release is not clean. 27 promoted beside one un-applied is rc 1.
#        · a REFUSED or never-completed move (`✗ … move failed (left in place)` /
#          `move NOT CONFIRMED`, the `<n> failed` count) — only on a run where NOTHING moved,
#          NOTHING was already released and NO program parent was withheld (each one withheld
#          would otherwise have been PATCHed, since the withhold is the last check). A refusal beside
#          a promoted card exits 0, as it always has: that is the card#9301 acceptance,
#          left standing deliberately (widening it changes runs this tool has always
#          called green). ⛔ AN UNREADABLE CARD BESIDE IT DOES NOT
#          DEMOTE IT TO 3: the refusal is a measurement, and the rule below is unscoped. That
#          pair is driven in `tests/promote-move-readback-selftest.sh` § 6.
#          ⛔ A GATE THAT MUST CATCH THAT ONE READS THE `<n> failed.` TAIL OF THE SUMMARY LINE,
#          NOT THE RC.
#   2  bad invocation, or a refusal this tool `die`s on before/instead of promoting — including
#      the REF-COMPLETENESS die: 0 cards newly promoted and no program parent withheld, behind a
#      NON-MERGE release tip, where a squash/rebase merge may have dropped the per-PR refs. ⛔ THAT
#      DIE IS NOT THE `--require-complete` GATE, which is rung 5: this one asks whether the REFS
#      this run reads arrived intact, that one asks whether a matched card's WORK is finished. They
#      were both called "the completeness die" while they lived on separate branches; they are
#      different questions at different exit codes and only the ref one is here. Both read-back
#      clauses above are decided BEFORE that die, because on their runs the refs demonstrably
#      arrived, matched cards and were PATCHed — sending the operator after dropped refs would be
#      the wrong question at an rc that means bad invocation.
#   3  $RC_UNVERIFIED — a stage PATCH WAS SENT, answered success, and its outcome could NOT be
#      read. Nothing here says it landed and nothing says it did not; re-read before acting
#      either way. It fires with `moved` > 0 too: 27 cards read back moved and one not read at
#      all is an incomplete record, and rc 0 would be this tool saying otherwise.
#      ⚠ A RED PROMOTE STEP IS NOT ALWAYS A FAILED ONE. Re-running is safe — a card already at
#      the target stage is skipped as already-released.
#   5  at least one matched card was REFUSED by the `--require-complete` completeness gate
#      (card#10176) — a `program`-tagged parent included: the gate runs before the withhold, so
#      such a parent is refused and counted `completeness-refused` like any card — ONLY REACHABLE
#      UNDER THAT FLAG, so no run that was possible before it can
#      reach this code. The card stayed in its shipped stage; nothing was written for it. TWO
#      VERDICT FAMILIES LAND HERE AND THE LINE SAYS WHICH: `INCOMPLETE` — a PR this release does
#      not contain still carries the card's work; and `UNMEASURED` — the oracle could not answer
#      (absent, not executable, refused to run, or silent for that card). They share an rc
#      deliberately: "no unreleased PR names this card" and "the PRs could not be read" are the
#      same silence, and only one of them licenses a terminal move, so the tool refuses on both
#      rather than guessing which it has. The number is `card-completeness`'s own rc 5, adopted
#      so the oracle and its caller do not disagree about what 5 means.
#      ⚠ IT IS LAST ON THE LADDER, BELOW 1, 2 AND 3, and that ranking is the point: those three
#      mean "this run is DEGRADED", 5 means "this run did the right thing and a human must land,
#      close or release something". A run carrying both reports the degradation, because 5 would
#      hide it. The remedy is on the gate's own FAILED line, which names the cards.
#      ⛔ 5 IS THE GATE FIRING, NOT THE GATE WORKING. Its absence proves nothing: a gate handed
#      the wrong release head / integration branch pair refuses nothing and exits 0,
#      indistinguishable from a clean release. Read `completeness-gate ON (…)` on stderr — it
#      names the pair it measured — before reading a 0 here as "every card is complete".
#   ⛔ 1 OUTRANKS 3 where one run carries both: 3 says NOBODY KNOWS, 1 is a MEASUREMENT that the
#   write did not take, and a run that has measured one does not hedge. Both run-level lines are
#   printed before the exit, so the cards to go and re-read are still named either way.
#
#   ⚠ WHAT THE `NOT APPLIED` RULING NEEDS FROM THE FAR END, AND CANNOT CHECK HERE. `not-applied`
#   — the one outcome that reds a release unconditionally — is decided from ONE `GET
#   /tasks/<id>.json` issued immediately after the PATCH, with no retry and no second look. That
#   GET is a MEASUREMENT only if the board answers it READ-AFTER-WRITE CONSISTENTLY for a
#   `workflow_stage_id` write. THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THE BOARD DEPLOYMENT, NOT OF THIS REPO, AND
#   NOTHING HERE ESTABLISHES IT: every test of this tool drives a stubbed transport, so no check
#   in this tree can reach the question. It is named rather than assumed either way. If a
#   deployment serves that read from a lagging replica or a cache, rc 1 and `move NOT APPLIED`
#   are reachable on a promotion that in fact landed, and the line's remedy ("promote them by
#   hand") is then wrong — on such a board read rc 1 as "go and re-read these cards", not as
#   "the move failed". `bin/kbcard`'s twin does NOT carry this precondition, and the asymmetry is
#   exactly where the assumption enters: it rules on the server's own write ECHO, inside the same
#   request, so it needs no second read to be consistent with the first.
#
# Usage: promote-released-cards [--dls LIST] [--cards IDS] [--base REF] [--head REF] [--source OWNER/REPO|*] [--shipped-stages IDS] [--require-complete] [--completeness PATH] [--dry-run] [--config PATH]
#
# --source "owner/repo" | "*": override `.promote.source` for this run (see REPO QUALIFICATION
#   above). One of the two — the config key or this flag — MUST be present, or the run refuses
#   before it reads the board. ABSENT is the only way to select the config value: an explicitly
#   EMPTY `--source ""` dies, like every other value-taking flag here, so an unexpanded shell
#   variable can never silently fall through to whatever the committed config happens to say.
#
# --cards "12,34": comma-separated numeric CARD IDS, correlated against each card's own
#   `id` (not against dl_number/pr_number). Opt-in and additive — ABSENT is the only way
#   to select the prior behavior, and with it absent every run is byte-identical to
#   before. Combinable with --dls; either flag selects the explicit-ref path, so no git
#   range is read. Strictly numeric: `--cards "card#12"` is a config error, not a parse.
#
# --base REF / --head REF: the ends of the git range the shipped refs are derived from, when
#   no explicit --dls/--cards set is given. Each is the caller's override for a leg this tool
#   otherwise derives, and each MUST NAME A COMMIT THIS REPO HAS: a ref that does not resolve is
#   refused at rc 2 naming the flag and the value, never read as an empty range (card#7535).
#
# --shipped-stages "51,52": comma-separated numeric workflow_stage_ids that count as a
#   "Shipped-class" source stage. When set, a DL/PR-matched card whose CURRENT stage is
#   NOT in this set is SKIPPED (with a log line) rather than moved — promotion means
#   Shipped→Released and must never resurrect a declined/backlog card carrying a stale or
#   recycled DL/PR stamp. Absent (default) = no source-stage guard, byte-identical prior
#   behavior — consumers opt in. ABSENT is the only way to select that default: an
#   explicitly EMPTY value (`--shipped-stages ""`) is a config error and dies, like any
#   other malformed set — it never silently degrades to the unguarded default.
#
# --require-complete: the COMPLETENESS GATE (toolkit card#10176; built as framework card#9869).
#   A matched card is REFUSED, not promoted, while the head branch of a PR this release does
#   not contain still names it — a PR still OPEN, or one MERGED to the integration branch
#   (`.release-pr.json` `.dev_branch`, default `dev`) by a commit the release head lacks — and
#   refused just the same when those PRs cannot be READ at all. Absent (default) = no gate,
#   byte-identical prior behavior — consumers opt in, exactly as they do for --shipped-stages.
#
#   WHY, AND WHY --shipped-stages DOES NOT COVER IT. A card's `pr_number` holds ONE PR, so a
#   card whose work spans several reaches its shipped stage on whichever part merged first, and
#   this mover then selects it by the ref that shipped and moves it to the TERMINAL released
#   stage — the one nobody re-reads — while its other parts are still open or unreleased. The
#   stage guard cannot help: it refuses cards OUTSIDE the shipped set, and such a card is
#   legitimately inside it.
#
#   THE PREDICATE IS NOT DEFINED HERE. It lives in ONE program, `card-completeness`, vendored
#   beside this one, together with every alternative it rejects and everything it cannot see
#   (read its header before relying on the gate — a DL-only branch, for one, is invisible to
#   it). It reads GitHub, so it needs $GITHUB_TOKEN (or $GH_TOKEN) with `pull-requests: read`
#   and `contents: read`; this script reads neither variable itself. A refused card exits at the
#   completeness rung of EXIT CODES above — which states what that rc is and what it is
#   conditional on, and is not restated here — and a refusal is printed per card, naming the PRs.
#
#   ⚠ READ THE GATE'S STATE LINE BEFORE TRUSTING `0 completeness-refused`. It names the RELEASE
#   HEAD and the INTEGRATION BRANCH the gate measured, and the channel each came from, because
#   those two values ARE the population: a gate asked about the wrong pair — no `--head` in a
#   checkout that is not the release, or a `.dev_branch` edited in the PR under review — finds
#   nothing to refuse and exits 0, which is indistinguishable from a clean release anywhere else
#   in the output.
#
# --completeness PATH: where that oracle is. Default: `card-completeness` beside this script.
#   Only consulted under --require-complete. An oracle that is absent or not executable is an
#   UNANSWERED QUESTION, never an absent problem: every matched card is refused and says so.
#
# ENVIRONMENT (all three are read at run time; none has a baked default):
#
# --- KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN (REQUIRED) --------------------------------------------------
#   The bearer token the card PATCHes are made with. Absent => refuse before any request.
#
# --- KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST (REQUIRED) ----------------------------------------------------
#   The one host that token may be sent to. The resolved api_base must be https:// on it, or
#   on a subdomain of it; anything else refuses before the first request. Pin it in the
#   promote CI env as a repo/org VARIABLE, not in the repo.
#
# --- KANBAN_API_BASE (optional) ---------------------------------------------------------
#   The api base to promote against, e.g. https://kanban.example.com/api/v3. It OVERRIDES
#   `.promote.api_base`; unset or empty means "use the committed value".
#
#   WHY IT EXISTS. The committed `.promote.api_base` is normally a host-scrubbed placeholder
#   — the real kanban host does not belong in a repo, least of all a vendored one — so the
#   config alone could name the guard's CONSTRAINT ($KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST, out-of-band) but
#   not its TARGET, and the two could never agree: every run refused, for everyone, and the
#   only ways to run the tool were to commit the real host or carry a permanent uncommitted
#   edit to a security-sensitive file (card#7494).
#
#   WHY IT IS NOT A HOLE IN THE GUARD. The value is host-validated by exactly the same
#   host_ok call as the config value — one resolution, one guard, no second path — so an
#   env-supplied base pointing off $KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST refuses identically. An env var is
#   not trusted here for being an env var; it is trusted for the same reason the config
#   value is, which is that it survived the guard. What being out-of-band buys is the
#   ATTACKER side: a PR can edit `.release-pr.json`, so it could always move the target and
#   never the constraint; with both supplied from the environment it can move NEITHER.
set -euo pipefail

die() { echo "promote-released-cards: $*" >&2; exit 2; }
for c in jq curl git; do command -v "$c" >/dev/null || die "$c is required"; done

# require_value <flag> <value>: die unless a value-taking flag was given a non-empty value.
# An option that consumes "$2" must dispatch on the FLAG being SEEN, not on its VALUE being
# non-empty — otherwise `--flag ""` (an unexpanded shell variable, an empty CI input) is
# indistinguishable from the flag being absent and silently selects the DEFAULT path. For
# --shipped-stages that default is "no source-stage guard", so an empty value produced an
# UNGUARDED promote with a summary line byte-identical to a guarded one (card#5144). None of
# these flags has a meaningful empty value; an intentional "none" would be a spelled
# sentinel. Also catches a trailing flag with no argument at all, which `set -u` would
# otherwise report as an unbound-variable error naming no flag.
# Mirrors kb_require_value in _kb-board-lib.sh (card#5146) — this script is vendored
# standalone into consumer repos and MUST NOT source that lib, so the guard is duplicated, as
# host_ok and numlist are; release-pr-body, release-artifacts-check and release-tag-check carry
# the same copy for the same reason. THE AGREEMENT IS CHECKED, NOT ASKED FOR (card#8529):
# `tests/mirror-pair-parity-selftest.sh` drives EVERY copy against the lib's over one corpus
# and derives the copy set from the tree, so a fifth one is compared on the day it lands.
require_value() { [ -n "${2:-}" ] || die "$1 requires a non-empty value"; }

# in_shipped_stage <stage-id>: true iff the id is in the comma-separated $SHIPPED_STAGES set.
# Only consulted when SHIPPED_STAGES is non-empty (the guard is opt-in).
in_shipped_stage() { case ",$SHIPPED_STAGES," in *",$1,"*) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac; }

# The tag that marks a card as a PARENT naming several legs — THE ONE PLACE THIS STRING IS
# WRITTEN in this script. The correlation's jq reads it through `--arg ptag`, the report lines
# interpolate it, and `tests/promote-program-withhold-selftest.sh` reads it back OUT of this line
# rather than restating it, so the spelling the withhold matches cannot drift from the spelling
# the operator is told about. Why the tag at all, why an exact match, and why a withhold rather
# than a refusal: THE PROGRAM-PARENT WITHHOLD in the header, which `--help` prints.
PROGRAM_TAG='program'

# uint_ok <s> / uint_csv_ok <s>: true iff <s> is a non-empty run of ASCII digits, or a
# non-empty comma-separated list of them (no leading/trailing/doubled comma).
#
# WHY THE LC_ALL — the non-obvious constraint. A bracket RANGE in a bash pattern is a
# COLLATION range, not an ASCII range: it means "every character that sorts between these
# two in the CURRENT locale". Under an ordinary en_US.UTF-8 shell, `case $x in *[!0-9]*)`
# does NOT fire for U+0663 ARABIC-INDIC DIGIT THREE — measured — so a board_id /
# released_stage_id / --shipped-stages / meta.total carrying one passed a check whose
# message says "numeric". Writing the class out longhand also works; LC_ALL=C is used
# because it is one mechanism for both the digit and CSV shapes. `local` scopes it to the
# call and restores it on return.
#
# ⛔ WHAT IS SHARED WITH `kb_is_uint` IN `_kb-board-lib.sh`, AND WHAT IS NOT. This comment used to
# say "keep the two in sync, as with require_value and host_ok", and that claim was FALSE on the
# accept set and had been since both were written — `kb_is_uint` is `^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$` and REFUSES
# a leading zero, this glob ACCEPTS one, so `007` was a board id here and not there. What the two
# genuinely share is the LC_ALL=C pin above and nothing else; `require_value` and `host_ok` really
# are copy-for-copy mirrors, and citing them here read as a claim of the same relationship.
#
# THE DIVERGENCE IS LEFT STANDING, NOT RULED ON HERE — closing it would narrow what this tool
# ACCEPTS, which is its own decision. Its direction is stated rather than waved at: a leading zero
# reaching these guards fails toward REFUSING, never toward a wrong promotion. `--shipped-stages
# 007` is admitted here and then matches no card, because `in_shipped_stage` compares it as text
# against the numeric `workflow_stage_id` the board returns, so the source-stage guard holds every
# card back and says so; `.promote.board_id` / `.promote.released_stage_id` with one are admitted
# and then addressed as written, against a board that answers or does not. What IS fixed is the
# CLAIM: the divergence is pinned as a value rather than asserted in prose.
# `tests/mirror-pair-parity-selftest.sh` § 3 drives both over one corpus, agrees row for row and
# names the two leading-zero rows on which they may differ, so a third divergence reds.
# `tests/locale-range-guard-selftest.sh` owns the LOCALE leg and drives both under both locales.
uint_ok()     { local LC_ALL=C; case "${1:-}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac; }
uint_csv_ok() { local LC_ALL=C; case "${1:-}" in ''|*[!0-9,]*|,*|*,|*,,*) return 1 ;; esac; }

# src_charset_ok <s>: true iff <s> holds only characters a GitHub `<owner>/<repo>` can hold —
# ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-`, and the separating `/`. It is a CHARSET check only; the
# `<owner>/<repo>` SHAPE is checked separately at the call site, and both are needed: the shape
# alone accepts `git@github.com:acme/widget` (one slash, two non-empty parts) — an scp-style
# remote spelling that no card's derived source can ever equal, so it correlates to nothing
# while looking configured. Same LC_ALL=C reasoning as uint_ok above, and it is not theoretical
# here: a bracket RANGE is a COLLATION range, so under en_US.UTF-8 this set silently admits
# characters outside ASCII. MEASURED, not asserted: with the pin removed, `--source
# acme/widg<U+0663>t` is ACCEPTED under en_US.utf8 and the run proceeds to the token guard.
# The covering arms are named, because a citation of "the class" is what this pin carried
# until card#8421 while NOTHING reached the line — tests/locale-range-guard-selftest.sh
# `promote --source rejects a U+0663 in the repo name` (behaviour, both locales) and its
# static backstop, which since that card matches the GLOB spelling used here and not only `=~`.
src_charset_ok() { local LC_ALL=C; case "${1:-}" in ''|*[!a-zA-Z0-9._/-]*) return 1 ;; esac; }

# host_ok <api_base>: true iff api_base is https:// and its host is $EXPECT_HOST or a
# subdomain of it. Assumes $EXPECT_HOST is non-empty (the caller enforces that before
# calling — see the required-var check below). Host is parsed per RFC 3986 — the authority
# ends at the FIRST of '/', '?' or '#', then userinfo before the last '@' is stripped, then
# :port (but see the IP-literal case below, which ends at ']' instead) — so
# `https://good@evil/` (→ evil), `https://good.evil/`, and
# `https://evil#@good.host` (which terminating at '/' alone let through, because the
# userinfo strip then reached into the fragment while curl discarded it) are all rejected.
# The parser MUST agree with curl about where the authority ends. Mirrors
# kb_require_https_host in _kb-board-lib.sh — this script is vendored standalone into
# consumer repos and MUST NOT source that lib, so the guard is duplicated; keep the two in
# sync, INCLUDING the required-var check. (#3570)
host_ok() {
  case "$1" in https://*) ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
  local h="${1#https://}"; h="${h%%[/?#]*}"; h="${h##*@}"
  # The port strip CANNOT start at the first ':' — a bracketed IPv6 literal is nothing but
  # colons, so `[::1]:8080` cut there yields `[`, and the refusal then names `[` as the host
  # to declare, which matches EVERY bracketed authority. RFC 3986 ends the IP-literal at ']'
  # and curl agrees, so the host runs through the FIRST ']'. An unclosed '[' keeps the plain
  # strip: curl rejects such a URL outright, so nothing is ever sent with it.
  case "$h" in
    \[*\]*) h="${h%%\]*}]" ;;
    *)      h="${h%%:*}" ;;
  esac
  # ONE trailing dot on either side, not a run: `h.` is the absolute spelling of the same
  # name and resolves identically, while `h..` is not a name at all and must keep failing.
  h="${h%.}"
  # Idempotent: the caller already strips the declared side, so this re-strip is a no-op
  # there. It is here because tests/kb-host-guard-selftest.sh lifts this function OUT of
  # the script and exercises it alone — the extracted unit must carry the whole policy.
  local e="${EXPECT_HOST%.}"
  [ -n "$h" ] || return 1
  # NOT dead code, and NOT a guard the caller already makes. The caller dies on an empty
  # EXPECT_HOST, but it strips the dot BEFORE that test, so the empty case cannot reach here
  # through the script — while the extracted unit has no caller at all. Without this, an
  # EXPECT_HOST of "." leaves e empty and the subdomain arm below becomes `*"."`, which
  # MATCHES any host that still ends in a dot (`h..` strips to `h.`): the unit ACCEPTS where
  # kb_require_https_host REFUSES, and "the two copies agree on every row" is the one thing
  # the selftest exists to assert. Fail closed on a declaration that normalised to nothing.
  [ -n "$e" ] || return 1
  [ "$h" = "$e" ] && return 0
  case "$h" in *".$e") return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
}

# redact_userinfo <url>: the url with any RFC 3986 USERINFO component replaced by `***`,
# everything else byte-identical; a url with no userinfo comes back UNCHANGED. Mirrors
# kb_redact_url_userinfo in _kb-board-lib.sh — this script is vendored standalone into
# consumer repos and MUST NOT source that lib, so the primitive is duplicated; keep the two
# in sync, as with host_ok and numlist. tests/kb-host-guard-selftest.sh lifts BOTH copies out
# and asserts they agree on every row, exactly as it does for host_ok. (card#7500)
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. host_ok ACCEPTS a base carrying userinfo — `https://u:pw@<expected-host>/`
# is a legitimate api_base and is a pinned accept row — so every message that echoed $API
# printed a password to stderr, i.e. into the CI log of the run that failed. The mask keeps
# the HOST, which is the only thing those messages exist to tell an operator.
#
# ⛔ THE AUTHORITY BOUNDARY IS host_ok's, and it must be. `https://evil.example#@good.host`
# has NO userinfo — the '@' is in the fragment, curl drops the fragment, the authority is
# evil.example, and host_ok refuses it (the #4346 row). Cutting at the last '@' anywhere in
# the string would rewrite that refusal to name good.host as the base being contacted.
redact_userinfo() {
  local u="$1" scheme="" auth rest
  # Scheme stripped only when the string actually STARTS with one: a bare `${u#*://}` matches
  # the FIRST `://` anywhere, so a schemeless `host/p?x://y` would lose everything up to it.
  # ⛔ LC_ALL IS PINNED ON THE LINE BELOW, NOT THREE LINES UP WITH THE OTHER `local`s. A bare
  # bracket RANGE widens under UTF-8 collation, so the pin is required — and
  # tests/locale-range-guard-selftest.sh's static backstop looks for `LC_ALL=C` on the guard's
  # own line or the two above it, which is a WRITING RULE, not a hint: with the pin declared up
  # with the other locals the scanner flagged this line (watched red). The lib's copy gets the
  # same pin from kb_ere_match, which this standalone must not call.
  local LC_ALL=C
  if [[ "$u" =~ ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*:// ]]; then
    scheme="${u%%://*}://"; u="${u#*://}"
  fi
  auth="${u%%[/?#]*}"     # authority ends at the FIRST of / ? # (RFC 3986)
  rest="${u:${#auth}}"
  case "$auth" in
    *@*) auth="***@${auth##*@}" ;;
  esac
  printf '%s' "$scheme$auth$rest"
}

# True iff <ref> is a MERGE commit (>=2 parents). `git rev-list --parents -n1`
# prints "<sha> <parent1> <parent2>..." → >=3 tokens ⇒ merge. A merge-commit release
# tip means the range carries every feature/prep commit subject, so the ref-derivation
# was COMPLETE; a non-merge tip (squash/rebase) may have collapsed it. git failure
# (unknown ref / shallow graft) ⇒ non-merge (fail-loud-safe). CI checks out
# fetch-depth:0, so parents are real.
merge_commit_tip() {
  local line
  line="$(git rev-list --parents -n1 "$1" 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  [ "$(printf '%s' "$line" | wc -w)" -ge 3 ]
}

# require_resolvable <flag> <ref>: die unless <ref> names a commit in THIS repo.
#
# WHY, AND WHY ONLY ON THE EXPLICIT LEGS (card#7535). `git log "$RANGE"` in the derive path
# below runs under `2>/dev/null || true`, so git's rc 128 "bad object" became an EMPTY subject
# list, which is indistinguishable from a genuinely empty range: the run printed "no shipped
# refs in range — nothing to do" and exited 0. That is a PROMOTION that promoted nothing and
# reported success — the release's cards stay un-promoted and the log looks clean. Measured on
# the pre-fix tool: `--base <good> --head <typo>`, `--base <typo> --head <good>` and a
# `--base <typo>` with the head defaulted ALL exited 0 with that line and no PATCH.
#
# `^{commit}` IS LOAD-BEARING, not decoration. `git rev-parse --verify -q <40-hex>` exits 0 for
# a full-length hex string naming NO object — it verifies that the spelling can be turned into a
# raw object name, not that the object is here (measured, git 2.43.0) — while `git log` on it
# dies `bad object`. A sha copied off a rebased-away branch or an old PR IS the deleted-ref case,
# and a raw sha is the natural hand spelling of --head. Peeling asks the question the range
# actually needs: an annotated tag peels through, and anything `git log` could not use as a range
# end fails here too, so no ref that resolves today changes behaviour.
#
# Mirrors require_resolvable in bin/release-pr-body (card#7525) — that tool met the same class
# one release bin over. This script is vendored standalone into consumer repos and MUST NOT
# source _kb-board-lib.sh, so the predicate is duplicated, as host_ok / require_value / numlist
# already are. The MESSAGES diverge on purpose: each names what its own tool does with a dead
# range, which is the half an operator acts on. What must not diverge is the VERDICT, and since
# card#8529 that is checked directly rather than asked for: tests/mirror-pair-parity-selftest.sh
# extracts BOTH copies and runs one corpus of refs — a live sha, HEAD, an annotated tag, a 40-hex
# non-object, a missing ref, an empty ref — through each, and reds when they disagree. Each tool's
# own selftest keeps its hex-sha arm, which reds if THAT copy loses the peel; the pair test is what
# reds when a NEW input class is handled differently by each.
require_resolvable() { # <flag> <ref>
  git rev-parse --verify -q "$2^{commit}" >/dev/null \
    || die "$1 '$2' does not resolve to a commit in this repo — a range built from it walks NOTHING, which this tool would report as 'no shipped refs in range — nothing to do' at rc 0: a promotion that promoted nothing and called it success. Check the spelling, or fetch the ref before passing it"
}

CONFIG=".release-pr.json"; DLS_IN=""; CARDS_IN=""; BASE=""; HEAD_REF=""; HEAD_SRC=""; DRY=0; SHIPPED_STAGES=""
SOURCE_IN=""; REQUIRE_COMPLETE=0; COMPLETENESS=""
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --dls)            require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; DLS_IN="$2"; shift 2;;
    --cards)          require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; CARDS_IN="$2"; shift 2;;
    --base)           require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; BASE="$2"; shift 2;;
    # HEAD_SRC records the CHANNEL, not the value, and is read only by the completeness gate's
    # state line: HEAD_REF is defaulted to `HEAD` further down, after which nothing can tell a
    # caller-supplied `--head HEAD` from a run that passed no --head at all.
    --head)           require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; HEAD_REF="$2"; HEAD_SRC="--head"; shift 2;;
    --source)         require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; SOURCE_IN="$2"; shift 2;;
    --shipped-stages) require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; SHIPPED_STAGES="$2"; shift 2;;
    --require-complete) REQUIRE_COMPLETE=1; shift;;
    --completeness)   require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; COMPLETENESS="$2"; shift 2;;
    --dry-run)        DRY=1; shift;;
    --config)         require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; CONFIG="$2"; shift 2;;
    # Print the contiguous leading comment block, whatever its length. A FIXED line range
    # silently truncates the moment the header outgrows it — this header reached 43 lines
    # while the range still said 20, so --help stopped before `Usage:`, the one block a
    # --help user needs. Widening the range is not the fix either: it would leak
    # `set -euo pipefail` and the helpers into user-facing output.
    -h|--help) awk 'NR>1 { if (substr($0,1,1) == "#") print; else exit }' "$0"; exit 0;;
    *) die "unknown arg '$1'";;
  esac
done

# Normalize + validate the opt-in source-stage guard set: strip whitespace, then require a
# non-empty comma-separated list of positive integers (mirrors the board_id/stage numeric
# checks). A malformed set is a config error, not a silent no-guard — fail loud.
# require_value has already rejected an explicitly-empty value, so a non-empty
# $SHIPPED_STAGES here means exactly "the flag was passed". The '' arm below is NOT dead
# code: a whitespace-only value (`--shipped-stages " "`) survives require_value and
# normalizes to empty right here, which is the case that arm exists to reject.
if [ -n "$SHIPPED_STAGES" ]; then
  SHIPPED_STAGES="$(printf '%s' "$SHIPPED_STAGES" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
  uint_csv_ok "$SHIPPED_STAGES" \
    || die "--shipped-stages must be a comma-separated list of numeric stage ids (got '$SHIPPED_STAGES')"
fi

# Same normalize-then-validate rule for --cards, and STRICT on purpose. --dls is a token
# stream that gets grepped, so junk in it degrades to "matched nothing"; --cards is an id
# list where junk that greps down to a number would move a card nobody named. A token
# spelling (`card#12`), a `#`, or anything else non-numeric is a config error here.
if [ -n "$CARDS_IN" ]; then
  CARDS_IN="$(printf '%s' "$CARDS_IN" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
  uint_csv_ok "$CARDS_IN" \
    || die "--cards must be a comma-separated list of numeric card ids, e.g. '5877,5874' (got '$CARDS_IN')"
fi

# SECURITY: .release-pr.json is security-sensitive — the api_base it names is where the
# writeback token (KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN) is sent. A PR editing api_base to point at an
# attacker host would exfiltrate the token on the next promote CI run. board_id/stage are
# validated numeric below; the RESOLVED api_base — whichever source it came from — is
# host-validated (https + $EXPECT_HOST) before any token is sent (#3570). Treat any api_base
# change in a PR as a credential-scope change.
[ -f "$CONFIG" ] || die "no $CONFIG in this repo"
cfg() { jq -r "$1 // empty" "$CONFIG"; }
BOARD="$(cfg '.promote.board_id')"
STAGE="$(cfg '.promote.released_stage_id')"
REF_RE="$(cfg '.ref_token_regex')"; REF_RE="${REF_RE:-DL-[0-9]+}"

# api_base: $KANBAN_API_BASE FIRST, else the committed .promote.api_base. ONE resolution
# feeding ONE guard — see the ENVIRONMENT block in the header for why the env channel exists
# and why it does not weaken the guard. $API_SRC only ever names the CHANNEL, so reporting it
# discloses nothing the api_base itself does not.
#
# EMPTY == UNSET here, and that is not the card#5144 shape. For a value-taking FLAG, an empty
# value had to become a hard error because it silently selected a DEFAULT PATH that was weaker
# than the flag (an unguarded promote reported as a guarded one). The default path here is the
# committed config value, which goes through the identical host guard — so the worst an empty
# $KANBAN_API_BASE can produce is a REFUSAL on the committed placeholder, never a token sent
# somewhere unvalidated. Treating it as unset is also what the CI wrapper needs: an unset
# repo/org variable renders as the empty string, and "no override" is the correct reading of it.
API="${KANBAN_API_BASE:-}"; API_SRC="\$KANBAN_API_BASE"
if [ -z "$API" ]; then API="$(cfg '.promote.api_base')"; API_SRC="$CONFIG .promote.api_base"; fi

[ -n "$BOARD" ] && [ -n "$STAGE" ] \
  || die ".promote.{board_id,released_stage_id} are required in $CONFIG"
[ -n "$API" ] \
  || die "no api base — set \$KANBAN_API_BASE, or .promote.api_base in $CONFIG"
uint_ok "$BOARD" || die ".promote.board_id must be numeric (got '$BOARD')"
uint_ok "$STAGE" || die ".promote.released_stage_id must be numeric (got '$STAGE')"

# --- REPO QUALIFICATION: resolve + validate `.promote.source` (card#8421) -----
# REQUIRED, no default, refused when absent — the REPO QUALIFICATION block in the header has
# the WHY (a bare dl/pr number is a repo-scoped counter, and this mover writes to a TERMINAL
# stage). Resolved HERE: after the config exists and its other `.promote` keys are validated,
# and BEFORE the token guard and every read, so a config that cannot say which repo it means
# never reaches a board at all.
#
# TWO SOURCES, ONE RESOLUTION, reported by CHANNEL exactly as $KANBAN_API_BASE is above.
# `--source` wins. A non-empty $SOURCE_IN means precisely "the flag was passed": require_value
# has already refused an empty value, and nothing else writes the variable — so a `${X:-$(cfg
# …)}` fall-through, which would run an UNGUARDED promote off a `"*"` config whenever an
# unexpanded variable reached the flag, cannot happen here.
#
# THE SHELL SPELLING OF `canonicalizeSource` IS `src_canon`, ONE FUNCTION, and BOTH shell-side
# values that take part in a source comparison go through it — the operator's `.promote.source`
# resolved just below, and the $GITHUB_REPOSITORY it is checked against further down. It was two
# byte-identical inline copies until the second one was minted six lines under a comment
# claiming to enumerate every copy of this rule, and nothing red: the extraction is canon #5 at
# the second caller, and it is also what keeps that census true by construction rather than by
# hand.
#
# The rule: trim -> empty means absent -> cap 255 -> lowercase, so the shell side and the jq
# side of the one comparison this script makes agree. `tr` runs under LC_ALL=C: a GitHub
# `<owner>/<repo>` is ASCII by construction, and a locale-sensitive fold would be a second,
# divergent rule — the class tests/locale-range-guard-selftest.sh exists for. That the value
# reaching this `tr` really is ASCII is not assumed either: `src_charset_ok` above enforces it
# for the config side, and the `promote --source` arms in that file drive this flag end to end
# under both locales.
src_canon() {
  local v
  v="$(printf '%s' "${1:-}" | sed -e 's/^[[:space:]]*//' -e 's/[[:space:]]*$//')"
  printf '%s' "${v:0:255}" | LC_ALL=C tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'
}
#
# ⚠ MIRRORS OF `canonicalizeSource`, ALL OF THEM — keep in sync (card#8421), and the list is
# CHECKED rather than trusted: tests/promote-source-qualify-selftest.sh § 3d reds when a copy
# named here is renamed, moved or dropped, and § 3e drives all three over one corpus so a
# DIVERGENCE between them reds however it was spelled. ⛔ CATCHING AN UNNAMED FIFTH COPY IS
# BEST-EFFORT, and § 3d — not this comment — OWNS that scope: which lines its predicate admits,
# every admission it makes and what it still cannot see are stated there, once. Do not restate
# them here; a scope restated in two places is what went stale last time. The kanban
# server class is the SoT; the copies are `src_canon` above (the SHELL side — every shell-side
# value passes through it), the jq `canon_source` in the correlation below (the CARD side — this
# script must not source the lib, so it cannot share one implementation with either), and
# `_ata_canon_source` in `bin/adopt-to-dl`, which canonicalizes the same value where a card is
# STAMPED with it. What binds the config and card sides to each other is NOT this comment — see
# the jq def, which states it exactly.
if [ -n "$SOURCE_IN" ]; then SOURCE="$SOURCE_IN"; SOURCE_SRC="--source"
else                        SOURCE="$(cfg '.promote.source')"; SOURCE_SRC="$CONFIG .promote.source"; fi
#
# THE OPERATOR'S OWN SPELLING IS KEPT, because every refusal below has to be GREPPABLE by the
# person who wrote the value. A message naming only the canonical form is a message they cannot
# search for: the committed placeholder `"source": "REPLACE_ME"` reads back as `replace_me`,
# which appears nowhere in their config or their workflow file, so the one search that would
# find the line to edit comes up empty. `src_shown` below renders the raw value, and the
# canonical form ONLY when canonicalization actually changed it.
SOURCE_RAW="$SOURCE"
SOURCE="$(src_canon "$SOURCE")"
src_shown() {
  if [ "$SOURCE_RAW" = "$SOURCE" ]; then printf "'%s'" "$SOURCE_RAW"
  else printf "'%s' (canonicalized to '%s')" "$SOURCE_RAW" "$SOURCE"; fi
}
# What the REQUIRED refusal calls a value that canonicalized to nothing. "empty" is only true of
# a genuinely empty one: an operator who wrote "  " needs to be told their value was there and
# vanished in the trim, or the message describes a config they do not have.
SRC_EMPTY_SHOWN="empty"
if [ -n "$SOURCE_RAW" ]; then SRC_EMPTY_SHOWN="'$SOURCE_RAW', which is whitespace only"; fi
# THE REFUSAL NAMES ITS OWN FIX, in full. A hard failure whose message does not tell an
# operator how to satisfy it is a worse defect than the one it closes: every existing install
# breaks on this key until it declares one, so the message carries the key, the block it goes
# in, the file, and the fact that `"*"` is the single-repo answer that restores prior behavior.
[ -n "$SOURCE" ] || die "refusing to promote — \`.promote.source\` is REQUIRED and has no default. \`dl_number\`/\`pr_number\` are REPO-SCOPED counters: on a board tracking several repos they collide, and an unqualified match promotes ANOTHER repo's cards into a terminal stage (measured: 15 shipped PRs matched 24 cards across 3 repos). TO FIX, add one key to the \`promote\` block of $CONFIG: \"source\": \"<owner>/<repo>\" naming the repo this release ships, OR \"source\": \"*\" to DECLARE that this board tracks exactly ONE repo — \"*\" is the single-repo answer and restores the previous behavior byte for byte. \`--source <owner/repo>|*\` overrides it for one run. (The value resolved from $SOURCE_SRC was $SRC_EMPTY_SHOWN.)"
if [ "$SOURCE" != '*' ]; then
  # Boundary validation of an operator-supplied value (canon #6). Every refusal below is a
  # spelling that would otherwise correlate to NOTHING while LOOKING configured — a silent
  # zero-promotion release, which is the failure this whole block exists to make impossible:
  #   - a URL: a card's derived source is `<owner>/<repo>`, never a scheme;
  #   - a `.git` suffix: `canonicalizeSource` does NOT trim it (only the URL path does, upstream),
  #     so `owner/repo.git` can never equal a card's URL-derived `owner/repo`;
  #   - a glob: `*` is a DECLARATION spelled alone, never a pattern — `owner/*` matches no card;
  #   - a bare name, >2 segments, an empty segment, or embedded whitespace: not the
  #     `<owner>/<repo>` any card resolves to.
  case "$SOURCE" in
    *://*)       die "--source/.promote.source must be a bare '<owner>/<repo>', not a URL (got $(src_shown), from $SOURCE_SRC)";;
    *.git)       die "--source/.promote.source must not carry a '.git' suffix — the source canonicalizer does not trim it, so $(src_shown) would never match a card's URL-derived 'owner/repo' (from $SOURCE_SRC)";;
    *\**)        die "--source/.promote.source is not a glob — '*' is the single-repo DECLARATION and is spelled alone; $(src_shown) would match no card (from $SOURCE_SRC)";;
    */*/*|/*|*/) die "--source/.promote.source must be exactly '<owner>/<repo>' (got $(src_shown), from $SOURCE_SRC)";;
    */*)         ;;
    *)           die "--source/.promote.source must be '<owner>/<repo>', or the single-repo declaration '*' (got $(src_shown), from $SOURCE_SRC)";;
  esac
  case "$SOURCE" in *[[:space:]]*) die "--source/.promote.source must not contain whitespace (got $(src_shown), from $SOURCE_SRC)";; esac
  # THE SHAPE `case` ABOVE AND THIS CHARSET CHECK ARE ONE PAIR, and neither half stands alone —
  # the shape accepts `git@github.com:acme/widget` (one slash, two non-empty parts) and the
  # charset accepts `acme/a/b`. `kb_is_repo_slug` in `bin/_kb-board-lib.sh` gates the SAME value
  # at the other end of the same correlation (it becomes the `<owner>/<repo>` of the pr_url
  # `bin/adopt-to-dl` stamps, and the `source=` of the by-ref verify) and is the ONE accept
  # predicate for a repo slug; this pair is its deliberate duplicate, because this script is
  # vendored standalone and must not source that lib. Keep the two in sync — and note the
  # spellings differ on purpose (that one is a single ERE plus a case-insensitive `.git` arm,
  # this one is a glob `case` pair over an already-lowercased value) while the ACCEPT SET must
  # not. THE EQUALITY IS A CONTRACT, NOT A CONVENTION: § 3c of
  # tests/promote-source-qualify-selftest.sh drives ONE corpus through both copies and pins each
  # side's verdict row for row, and the only two spellings on which they may differ (`*`, and a
  # value this script trims before judging) are declared there by name. It exists because the
  # comment that used to stand here — "keep the two in sync" — had already failed: the lib arm
  # was `*.git)`, case-SENSITIVE, so `acme/widget.GIT` was refused HERE and accepted THERE
  # (card#8421). tests/locale-range-guard-selftest.sh remains the LOCALE leg of the same pair,
  # driving both ends under both locales.
  src_charset_ok "$SOURCE" \
    || die "--source/.promote.source must be a bare '<owner>/<repo>' — only letters, digits and '.', '_', '-' may appear either side of the one '/'. $(src_shown) (from $SOURCE_SRC) carries something else, so it can never equal a card's derived 'owner/repo'; an scp-style 'git@github.com:owner/repo' is the usual cause."
fi
# --- THE CHECK HALF of the same seam: the declared source must BE this repo (card#8538) ----
# `.promote.source` is a DECLARATION made in one repo about which repo's cards a release
# promotes, and until now nothing anywhere established that the declaration was TRUE of the
# repo it was declared in. A typo'd or copy-pasted slug — `PupFuzz/kanban-boards`, or a slug
# lifted wholesale from the repo the config was copied from — passes the shape `case` and
# `src_charset_ok` above and then correlates against NO card: every release promotes 0, prints
# `⊘ … other-repo` lines nobody reads, and exits 0. A guard whose failure mode is a clean exit
# is not a guard, and the DECLARE half added in `release-artifacts-check` cannot close it —
# presence and shape are local facts, and "is this the right repo" is not.
#
# $GITHUB_REPOSITORY is the one thing in the environment that answers it: GitHub sets it to
# `<owner>/<repo>` of the repository the workflow is running in, so on the promote runner it is
# the repo whose release is being promoted, by construction. Compared through `src_canon` — the
# SAME function the config value went through above, not a second spelling of the rule — because
# the comparison is only worth something if both sides mean one thing (GitHub slugs are
# case-insensitive; a config saying `PupFuzz/kanban-board` and a runner saying
# `pupfuzz/kanban-board` are the same repo).
#
# THREE THINGS IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO:
#   * it does not fire on `*`, which is a DECLARATION that the board tracks one repo, not a
#     repo name — there is nothing to compare it against, and refusing it would break every
#     single-repo install for no finding;
#   * it does not fire when $GITHUB_REPOSITORY is unset or empty — off a runner (an operator's
#     terminal, a vendored cron) there is no repo identity to check against, and inventing one
#     from `git remote` would be a second, weaker rule that a fork or a mirror answers wrongly;
#   * it has NO override flag. `--source` is already the per-run override and this check applies
#     to whatever `--source` resolved to, deliberately: a run that promotes another repo's cards
#     is the failure, not the spelling that reached it. A fork running the release workflow is
#     the case that makes this a safety leg and not only a typo leg — its $GITHUB_REPOSITORY is
#     the fork, so it cannot promote the upstream board's cards on the upstream's ref numbers.
GH_REPO="${GITHUB_REPOSITORY:-}"
GH_REPO="$(src_canon "$GH_REPO")"
if [ "$SOURCE" != '*' ] && [ -n "$GH_REPO" ] && [ "$SOURCE" != "$GH_REPO" ]; then
  die "refusing to promote — the resolved repo qualification $(src_shown) (from $SOURCE_SRC) is not the repository this run is in (\$GITHUB_REPOSITORY = '$GH_REPO'). Every ref this release ships is a REPO-SCOPED number, so a source naming a different repo correlates against that repo's cards or against none at all: the run would exit 0 having promoted nothing, or having moved cards this release did not ship. TO FIX, set \`.promote.source\` in $CONFIG to '$GH_REPO' (or to \"*\" if this board tracks exactly ONE repo). If the mismatch is deliberate, this tool is the wrong one: it promotes the cards of the repo it runs in."
fi

# The jq comparison key: the canonical source, or "" meaning qualification is OFF. Everything
# downstream branches on THIS, never on $SOURCE, so `*` has exactly one meaning in one place.
if [ "$SOURCE" = '*' ]; then QUALIFY_SRC=""; else QUALIFY_SRC="$SOURCE"; fi

TOKEN="${KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN:-}"
[ -n "$TOKEN" ] || die "KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN is not set"
API="${API%/}"

# Fail-closed host guard on the RESOLVED api_base BEFORE any token is sent — one call, on
# whichever source won above, because two guarded paths is how one of them ends up unguarded.
# The expected host is $KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST — REQUIRED, no baked default: this script is
# vendored by operators onto their own kanban hosts, so there is no host to safely assume.
# Pin it in the promote CI env (a repo/org variable — out-of-band from the PR-editable
# .release-pr.json, so an attacker's api_base edit can't also change the constraint). If it
# is unset/empty we refuse BEFORE any token is sent.
# The single trailing dot comes off the DECLARED host here, before the non-empty check, so
# a declaration of "." alone normalises to empty and dies LOUD rather than becoming a
# wildcard — the same order kb_require_known_api_host uses in _kb-board-lib.sh.
# THE ONLY SPELLING OF THE RESOLVED BASE THAT MAY BE RENDERED, resolved once here — after
# the trailing-slash strip, so it is the final value — so every message below inherits the
# redaction rather than having to remember it (card#7500). $API itself is what goes on the
# wire; $API_SHOWN is what goes to stderr.
API_SHOWN="$(redact_userinfo "$API")"

EXPECT_HOST="${KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST:-}"; EXPECT_HOST="${EXPECT_HOST%.}"
[ -n "$EXPECT_HOST" ] \
  || die "KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST must be set to the expected api host before sending the writeback token; refusing to send"
host_ok "$API" \
  || die "refusing to send token — api_base '$API_SHOWN' (from $API_SRC) is not https:// on '$EXPECT_HOST' (or a subdomain). A malicious .release-pr.json api_base can exfiltrate KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN; set KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST to your kanban host, and \$KANBAN_API_BASE to the api base on it."

# State the CHANNEL the accepted api_base came from, every run. Which source won is the one
# thing a two-source resolution can get wrong that no other output reveals — a stale exported
# $KANBAN_API_BASE silently outranking a corrected config is a run against the wrong board
# with a clean summary. stderr, so the stdout summary line consumers parse stays byte-identical.
#
# THE CHANNEL AND THE EXPECTED HOST, NOT THE RESOLVED BASE — deliberately. host_ok ACCEPTS a
# base carrying userinfo (`https://u:pw@<expected-host>/api/v3` is a legitimate api_base and is
# asserted as accepted in tests/kb-host-guard-selftest.sh), so echoing $API on the SUCCESS path
# would print a credential into every CI run log, on every run. The two facts an operator
# actually needs — which channel won, and what it was checked against — carry neither.
# (The refuse paths print $API_SHOWN — the same base with any userinfo masked by
# redact_userinfo above, adopted at every render site in this tool and in the lib, card#7500.)
echo "promote-released-cards: api_base resolved from $API_SRC, host-guarded against '$EXPECT_HOST'" >&2

# --- resolve the shipped refs (DL tokens + PR numbers) -----------------------
# numlist normalizes the SHIPPED side (a git-log / --dls / --cards text stream) to bare
# integers with leading zeros stripped (keeping >=1 digit) + deduped. The CARD side is
# normalized by the `norm` jq def in the correlation below, re-expressed in jq because the two
# sides live in different runtimes (shell stream vs board JSON) on opposite sides of one
# comparison, and this script is a vendored standalone that must not source the lib.
#
# WHAT THE TWO SIDES SHARE, AND WHERE THEY DELIBERATELY DIVERGE — stated exactly, because the
# claim this comment used to make ("the SAME rule; keep the two in sync") was true of the
# leading-zero strip only and false of everything else (card#7587).
#   * SHARED, and a change to either must be mirrored: the leading-zero strip. "093" and 93
#     must compare equal, so `sed 's/^0*\([0-9]\)/\1/'` here and `sub("^0+(?=.)";"")` there
#     are one rule in two runtimes, like the host_ok mirror.
#   * DIVERGENT BY DESIGN on a value carrying SEVERAL digit runs. numlist SPLITS it — each run
#     becomes its own candidate ref. `norm` REFUSES it — the card correlates to nothing. The
#     asymmetry is the point: this side's value names what SHIPPED and every feed into it is
#     already single-run (`--cards` is uint_csv_ok-validated; the PR marker is `\(#[0-9]+\)[[:space:]]*$`;
#     DL tokens are whatever `ref_token_regex` matched), while the CARD side's value has
#     arbitrary provenance and decides a PATCH on a card, so it fails safe. They agree
#     byte-for-byte on every single-run value, which is the only shape either side is
#     supposed to see. `tests/promote-ref-canon-selftest.sh` asserts both columns off one
#     shared fixture, so the divergence stays measured rather than assumed.
#
# ⚠ TWO WAYS THIS SIDE CAN STILL EMIT A REF THE CARD SIDE CANNOT MATCH OR DID NOT MEAN.
# Both are reported rather than closed here — narrowing this accept set changes which cards
# the sweep moves, which is its own ruling. The first OVER-emits (a spurious ref that can
# match an unrelated card); the second UNDER-emits (a ref nothing can equal, so a card that
# genuinely shipped is left un-promoted):
#   * a `ref_token_regex` whose own MATCH carries two digit runs ("PROJ2-DL-[0-9]+") makes
#     this split into a spurious extra ref ("2") that can match an unrelated card's
#     dl_number. The default `DL-[0-9]+` cannot; `bin/release-pr-body`'s emit_card_ids hit
#     the same shape and took the TRAILING run instead, which is the fix if this is ever
#     ruled on.
#   * `[0-9]` is a RANGE, so a UTF-8 collation widens it: measured under en_US.UTF-8,
#     "DL-4<U+0663>" yields the ref "4<U+0663>", which `norm`'s ASCII-only set cannot equal,
#     so the card is not promoted (see tests/locale-range-guard-selftest.sh for the class).
numlist() { grep -oE '[0-9]+' | sed 's/^0*\([0-9]\)/\1/' | awk 'NF && !seen[$0]++'; }
CARD_IDS="$(printf '%s' "$CARDS_IN" | numlist || true)"
if [ -n "$DLS_IN" ] || [ -n "$CARDS_IN" ]; then
  DL_NUMS="$(printf '%s' "$DLS_IN" | grep -oiE "$REF_RE" | numlist || true)"
  PR_NUMS=""
else
  # AN EXPLICIT --base/--head MUST RESOLVE HERE, and is checked BEFORE the range is built
  # (card#7535). Non-empty at this point means exactly "the flag was passed": require_value has
  # already rejected an empty value, and neither variable has any other writer.
  #
  # ⛔ ONLY THE EXPLICIT LEGS. A DEFAULTED HEAD_REF is `HEAD`, and it is deliberately NOT checked
  # here: its failure direction is the opposite one and is dispositioned in place — an empty repo
  # falls through to the base-tag die below, which names no flag because the caller passed none.
  # A DEFAULTED BASE is `git describe`'s answer about that same tip and gets the full-history-
  # sweep refusal below, which is the stronger check. Widening this guard to cover them would
  # rewrite two refusals that are already correct into ones that name flags nobody used.
  #
  # THE BASE LEG IS CHECKED FIRST, matching bin/release-pr-body's ordering so the two tools
  # answer the same way about the same pair of flags. The head leg must nonetheless be checked
  # before the `git describe "${HEAD_REF}^"` below, and is: that describe swallows its own
  # failure, leaving BASE empty, so an unresolvable EXPLICIT --head used to surface as
  # "no base tag found before <head>" — a refusal (rc 2) that blamed the wrong leg, and printed
  # a NOTE about a baseline of ''.
  [ -z "$BASE" ]     || require_resolvable --base "$BASE"
  [ -z "$HEAD_REF" ] || require_resolvable --head "$HEAD_REF"
  HEAD_REF="${HEAD_REF:-HEAD}"
  if [ -z "$BASE" ]; then
    BASE="$(git describe --tags --abbrev=0 "${HEAD_REF}^" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    # A DERIVED baseline uses LOCAL tags. The authoritative CI invocation fetches
    # tags (fetch-depth 0 + fetch-tags), so it is remote-truth by construction; a
    # LOCAL run's tag set can lag a release and silently over-report the shipped
    # range (card #4389 — the release-pr-body #4348 lie, one tool over). Name it
    # rather than fetching here: this is a vendored security tool whose behavior
    # must stay deterministic; the explicit override is --base.
    [ -n "${GITHUB_ACTIONS:-}" ] || echo "promote-released-cards: NOTE — baseline '$BASE' derived from LOCAL tags; if origin has newer release tags this range over-reports. Run 'git fetch --tags' first or pass --base <tag>." >&2
  fi
  # An empty base would make RANGE collapse to just HEAD → git log walks ALL history
  # → every DL/PR ever committed is "shipped" → mass wrong-card move. Refuse it (the
  # first-ever release, or a shallow/tagless clone). Use --base or --dls explicitly.
  [ -n "$BASE" ] || die "no base tag found before $HEAD_REF — refusing a full-history sweep; pass --base <ref> or --dls"
  RANGE="$BASE..$HEAD_REF"
  SUBJECTS="$(git log "$RANGE" --no-merges --format='%s' 2>/dev/null || true)"
  DL_NUMS="$(printf '%s\n' "$SUBJECTS" | grep -oiE "$REF_RE" | numlist || true)"
  # The squash-merge PR marker is the TRAILING (#NNN) — not the inline #card refs.
  PR_NUMS="$(printf '%s\n' "$SUBJECTS" | grep -oE '\(#[0-9]+\)[[:space:]]*$' | numlist || true)"
fi
[ -n "$DL_NUMS$PR_NUMS$CARD_IDS" ] || { echo "promote-released-cards: no shipped refs in range — nothing to do."; exit 0; }
jarr() { printf '%s' "$1" | jq -R . | jq -s 'map(select(. != ""))'; }
DLS_J="$(jarr "$DL_NUMS")"; PRS_J="$(jarr "$PR_NUMS")"; CARDS_J="$(jarr "$CARD_IDS")"

# --- read the WHOLE board (paged) --------------------------------------------
# Auth is fed OUT-OF-BAND via a stdin herestring (-H @- <<<) so the bearer token never
# appears in curl's argv (world-readable via `ps aux` / /proc/<pid>/cmdline on a multi-user
# host, #3569). A herestring also redirects a regular temp file onto fd 0 rather than a
# /dev/fd named pipe, so it works on native mingw64/Git-Bash curl where process substitution
# (-H @<(...)) fails to open its fd (#34); the temp is seekable, so curl's --retry is safe.
# --retry rides through a transient prod outage: the merge-to-main promote job
# races the operator's `bin/deploy.sh`, which drops prod into maintenance mode
# (HTTP 503) for the seconds-long deploy window (#3700). curl's --retry backs off
# exponentially on a transient error only — HTTP 408/429/5xx, a timeout, or (with
# --retry-connrefused) a refused connection — never a 4xx, so a genuine 401/404
# still fails fast. Bounded by --retry-max-time so a truly-down prod still exits.
# Card moves are idempotent (set stage N) and a 503 applies nothing, so retrying
# a PATCH is safe. THAT REASONING SURVIVES card#9301 UNCHANGED AND IS MEASURED, NOT
# INHERITED: `--retry` keys off the response status, not off `-f`, so dropping `-f`
# below does not disarm it — measured against a server answering 503, 503, 200 on one
# path, which took THREE server hits and returned the 200 body, while a 401 and a 422
# each took exactly ONE. ⚠ NO STUB CAN RE-MEASURE THAT — `--retry` is curl's own internal
# loop, so a stubbed curl IS the thing that would have to retry — which is why
# tests/promote-refusal-detail-selftest.sh § 4 pins the FLAG LINE (and the absence of `-f`)
# and carries the real-curl reproduction in its own text rather than faking the behaviour.

# --- THE api() FAILURE DETAIL (card#9301) --------------------------------------------------
# THE BOUND on a rendered excerpt of a server's error body, as a NAMED CONSTANT so a guard can
# pin it rather than a magic number: 400 bytes carries a whole validation envelope
# (`{"message":…,"errors":{…}}`) and is small enough that a proxy's branded HTML 502 cannot
# flood one per-card log line. tests/promote-refusal-detail-selftest.sh § 3 asserts BOTH
# directions against this name — a body under it is rendered whole, one over it is cut to
# exactly this many bytes and says so.
API_ERR_EXCERPT_MAX=400

# THE rc api() RETURNS WHEN THE SERVER ANSWERED AND REFUSED — as a NAME, because more than one
# site now depends on the SAME value meaning the same thing: api() returns it, and the move loop reads it to
# decide whether it may claim the card was left in place. 22 is curl's own `-f` rc for that state,
# and `-f` is gone (see api() below), so nothing else in this tool can produce it: without `--fail`
# curl documents 22 as unreachable. Every OTHER non-zero rc out of api() is curl's own transport rc
# (7 refused, 28 timeout, 35 TLS …), i.e. the state whose outcome is UNKNOWN.
API_RC_REFUSED=22

# THE THIRD WRITE OUTCOME, ADOPTED RATHER THAN MINTED (card#9938, card#8556). A write has three
# outcomes, not two: APPLIED, NOT APPLIED AND KNOWN, and SENT-BUT-UNREADABLE. `bin/kbcard` already
# owns that ladder for this fleet — its `WHAT A WRITE VERB'S EXIT CODE MEANS` block is the
# contract, and `KBC_RC_UNVERIFIED=3` is the value — so this tool uses THAT rc and THAT vocabulary
# instead of a second spelling of one outcome. This script is vendored standalone and must not
# source `_kb-board-lib.sh` (nor exec kbcard, which is not vendored with it), so the value is
# DUPLICATED, as require_value / host_ok / redact_userinfo are. The duplication is legitimate only
# because it is PINNED: `tests/mirror-pair-parity-selftest.sh` § 6 extracts both assignments from
# the two shipped files and reds when they differ, and drives this file's `stage_verdict` beside
# kbcard's `_kbc_confirm_stage` over one corpus.
#
# ⛔ THE LADDER IS NOT RESTATED HERE. It used to be — as `1  a KNOWN failure — refused, or the
# read-back says the card did not move.` — and that sentence declared a condition this file does
# not evaluate: a REFUSED move reaches rc 1 only on a run where nothing moved, nothing was
# already released and no program parent was withheld (the clause below the summary line), so
# a refusal beside a promoted card exits 0 and the line said otherwise. This block owns the VALUE
# and why it is duplicated; the header's EXIT CODES block (`--help`) owns what each rc MEANS and
# what it is conditional on.
RC_UNVERIFIED=3

# TWO FILES, NOT TWO VARIABLES, and the reason is shell scoping rather than taste. The
# dominant caller shape in this tool is `resp="$(api …)"` — a COMMAND SUBSTITUTION, i.e. a
# SUBSHELL — so anything api() assigns to a global there dies with the subshell and the
# parent's `die` never sees it. A file crosses that boundary; a variable cannot. The other
# caller (`if api -X PATCH … >/dev/null`) does run api() in the current shell, but throws
# stdout away, so a marker carried ON stdout cannot reach it either. One file serves both.
#   $API_BODY_FILE  curl's `-o` target, i.e. the response body verbatim.
#   $API_ERR_FILE   line 1 = the HTTP status the last api() call received, or `000` meaning
#                   NO answer was read at all; lines 2+ = that answer's body. Written on the
#                   FAILURE path only, and read only through resp_detail below.
API_BODY_FILE="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/promote-api-body.XXXXXX")" \
  || die "cannot create a temporary file for the api response body"
API_ERR_FILE="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/promote-api-detail.XXXXXX")" \
  || die "cannot create a temporary file for the api failure detail"
trap 'rm -f "$API_BODY_FILE" "$API_ERR_FILE"' EXIT

# api — the one request seam. Prints the response body on stdout and returns 0 when the call
# SUCCEEDED; on any failure prints nothing on stdout, records the status and the server's own
# body in $API_ERR_FILE for resp_detail, and returns non-zero.
#
# ⛔ `-f` IS GONE ON PURPOSE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE CARD. `curl -f` DISCARDS an HTTP error
# body, so by the time the move loop wanted to report a refusal there was nothing left to
# report: a 401 (token expired), a 403 (the role lacks `task.update`), a 409 (the card moved
# under us) and a 422 (wrong stage id for this board) all reached the operator as one
# sentence, and the one fact that separates them — the server's own explanation — had already
# been thrown away (card#9301).
#
# ⛔ AND DROPPING IT FLIPS curl's rc, WHICH IS THE HAZARD THE REST OF THIS FUNCTION EXISTS TO
# NEUTRALISE. Both callers gate on the rc (`resp="$(api …)" || die` and `if api -X PATCH …`),
# and WITHOUT `-f` curl exits 0 on a 401 — every one of those gates would have read a refusal
# as a SUCCESS. So the rc is re-derived here from the status, and it is reproduced EXACTLY,
# not merely "still non-zero". MEASURED on curl 8.5.0, `curl -fsS` against every status class:
#
#     101  rc 0                                      400  rc 22    500  rc 22
#     200  rc 0   201 rc 0   204 rc 0                401  rc 22    503  rc 22
#     301  rc 0   302 rc 0   304 rc 0                403  rc 22
#     307  rc 0   399 rc 0                           409  rc 22    422  rc 22
#
# ⚠ THE 1xx ROW USED TO READ `100  rc 1  (transport: HTTP/0.9 not allowed)`, WHICH CONTRADICTED
# THE `1` IN THE ARM BELOW — and the arm was right, the row was mislabelled. That measurement was
# not of a 1xx STATUS at all: it came from a response with no valid status line, which curl rejects
# as HTTP/0.9 BEFORE any code exists, so `%{http_code}` is `000` and the failure is a TRANSPORT
# one — handled by the `rc -ne 0` branch below, never by the status case. Re-measured on curl 8.5.0
# against a raw-socket server, each case driven separately:
#   * final status line `HTTP/1.1 101` → `%{http_code}` 101, `curl -fsS` rc 0 (a SUCCESS under
#     `-f`, which is what the `1` reproduces);
#   * `HTTP/1.1 100 Continue` then `HTTP/1.1 200` → `%{http_code}` 200, rc 0 — an INTERIM 1xx is
#     never the reported code, which is why a bare 100 is not reachable through `-w` at all;
#   * a bare body with no status line → `%{http_code}` `000`, rc 1, both with and without `-f`.
# So the table and the arm now agree, and nothing about what this tool ACCEPTS moved: narrowing to
# `[23]??` would have been a change to that, on a status curl reports as a `-f` success.
#
# ⚠ SO THE SHIPPED SUCCESS BOUNDARY IS 400, NOT 200 — `-f` fails at 400 and above and passes
# EVERY 3xx through as a success, body and all. `[123]??` below reproduces that, deliberately:
# a `2??` arm would be a NARROWING, turning a redirect (an http→https or trailing-slash proxy
# redirect is the ordinary way to meet one) from a success into a failure, i.e. a change to
# what this tool accepts — which is not this card's to make. It is not a silent hazard either:
# a 3xx body carries no card array, so on page 1 it reaches the ZERO-CARDS die below and on a
# later page the no-readable-card-array die — both refuse, neither promotes. ⚠ The page-1
# message is then WRONG ABOUT THE CAUSE (it names board membership), which is the already-known
# accepted collapse recorded at that arm for card#6630; a redirect is one more input that lands
# on it. The 3xx-reads-as-success question is REPORTED rather than closed here.
#
# ⛔ `-o FILE` RATHER THAN CAPTURING curl's STDOUT, and this is not a style choice either.
# With the body left on stdout, `--retry` CONCATENATES: measured against a server answering
# 503, 503, 200, the captured stdout held all THREE bodies back to back, which is not JSON and
# which `fetch_whole_board` would have fed to jq as a board. curl truncates its `-o` target at
# the start of each attempt, so the file holds the LAST answer only (measured: 139 bytes, the
# 200's body alone). That is also why `-f` could be dropped safely at all — it was `-f` that
# used to suppress the intermediate error bodies.
api() {
  local status rc=0
  # Truncate BOTH before the call. curl truncates its own `-o` target, but a curl that dies
  # before it opens a connection never gets there and would leave the PREVIOUS call's body
  # behind — so resp_detail would render one card's body under another card's status.
  : > "$API_BODY_FILE"; : > "$API_ERR_FILE"
  status="$(curl -sS --retry 5 --retry-connrefused --retry-max-time 60 \
                 -o "$API_BODY_FILE" -w '%{http_code}' \
                 -H @- -H "Accept: application/json" "$@" \
                 <<<"$(printf 'Authorization: Bearer %s' "$TOKEN")")" || rc=$?
  # THE REQUEST DID NOT COMPLETE: curl's own rc is propagated verbatim (7 refused, 28 timeout,
  # 35 TLS …), byte-identical to the pre-card#9301 behaviour, and curl's `-sS` message has
  # already gone to stderr. `000` is the status sentinel the lib's kb_api uses for the same
  # state, and it means the outcome of a WRITE is UNKNOWN — not that nothing was written.
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf '000\n' > "$API_ERR_FILE"; return "$rc"; fi
  case "$status" in
    [123]??) cat "$API_BODY_FILE"; return 0 ;;
  esac
  # The server ANSWERED and refused. $API_RC_REFUSED is curl's own `-f` rc for this state, kept
  # byte-identical so that a caller reading the NUMBER is unaffected — and the move loop below IS
  # such a caller: this rc is what separates "the server said no" from "we never heard back", and
  # only the first of those two lets a message claim the card is still where it was.
  { printf '%s\n' "$status"; cat "$API_BODY_FILE"; } > "$API_ERR_FILE"
  return "$API_RC_REFUSED"
}

# resp_detail — THE ONLY SPELLING OF A RENDERABLE api() FAILURE. Prints one line: the HTTP
# status the server answered with, plus a bounded, secret-masked excerpt of the server's OWN
# body. Every message that reports an api() failure interpolates THIS and nothing else, for
# exactly the reason $API_SHOWN is the only renderable spelling of the resolved base
# (card#7500): the next error path somebody adds in this tool then inherits the mask and the
# bound instead of having to remember them at a new call site.
#
# ⛔ THE MASK RUNS BEFORE THE BOUND, and the order is load-bearing. Cutting first can split a
# credential, and half a token no longer EQUALS $TOKEN — so the literal-match mask then finds
# nothing and the surviving prefix is printed. Scrub, then cut.
#
# ⛔ WHAT IS MASKED IS $TOKEN, AND NOT THE URL USERINFO — a measured decision, not an omission.
# The bearer token is the one secret a response body can actually come to contain: it goes out
# in a header, and a server rendering debug output echoes request headers into its own error
# page. MEASURED against a header-echoing server: the 422 body came back carrying
# `"Authorization": "Bearer <the token>"` verbatim. The api_base's userinfo cannot arrive by
# that route — curl does not put userinfo on the wire, and with api()'s explicit
# `-H "Authorization: Bearer …"` it does not convert it into a Basic header either (measured:
# the request line was `GET /tasks/1.json` and the only Authorization header the server saw
# was the Bearer one), so the server cannot echo a credential it never received.
# `redact_userinfo` is ALSO the wrong instrument for a body: it is an RFC 3986 AUTHORITY
# parser, so handed `{"a":"b@c"}` it returns `***@c"}` — it would mangle JSON while protecting
# nothing. The base itself is still rendered only as $API_SHOWN, unchanged.
resp_detail() {
  # LC_ALL=C pins the bound to BYTES, and the pin is MEASURED to work from inside the function
  # rather than assumed: `${#s}` and `${s:0:n}` are CHARACTER operations, so a 5-character
  # 15-byte body reads as 5 under an ambient en_US.UTF-8 and as 15 with this `local` in scope
  # (measured, bash 5.2.21) — without it $API_ERR_EXCERPT_MAX stops being one number and the
  # excerpt cuts at a different place per runner locale. Same class
  # tests/locale-range-guard-selftest.sh owns, same reason uint_ok carries its own pin.
  local LC_ALL=C all status body
  # ONE read, AND IT KEEPS ITS OWN OUTCOME. A `$(… 2>/dev/null || true)` here would collapse
  # "the server sent no body" into "this tool could not read back its own record" — which is
  # the read-outcome-collapse class (tests/read-outcome-collapse-selftest.sh), and it is the
  # SAME defect this card fixes one layer up, so re-minting it inside the fix would be absurd.
  # The status and the body are then split by parameter expansion, not by a second reader.
  all="$(cat "$API_ERR_FILE" 2>&1)" || {
    printf 'this tool could not read back its own record of the response (%s)' "$all"
    return 0
  }
  [ -n "$all" ] || { printf 'no HTTP outcome was recorded for this call'; return 0; }
  status="${all%%$'\n'*}"
  if [ "$status" = "000" ]; then
    printf 'the request DID NOT COMPLETE, so no HTTP status came back at all and whether the server received it is UNKNOWN (curl reported the transport error above)'
    return 0
  fi
  # A file holding ONLY the status line leaves status == all, i.e. the server sent no body.
  if [ "$status" = "$all" ]; then body=""; else body="${all#*$'\n'}"; fi
  body="${body//"$TOKEN"/***}"
  # One log LINE: newlines/tabs to spaces, then C0 controls AND DEL dropped, runs squeezed.
  # ⚠ C0 AND DEL, NOT C1 — the set below is `\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177`, so 0x80-0x9F
  # (C1, including 0x9B CSI) SURVIVES. That is a limit of this scrub, stated rather than claimed
  # away, and EXTENDING it is the wrong fix: `\200-\237` overlaps UTF-8's continuation-byte range
  # (0x80-0xBF), so adding it would corrupt legitimate multi-byte text. MEASURED on this box —
  # `printf 'caf\xc3\xa9 \xe2\x82\xac' | tr -d '\200-\237'` returns `caf\303\251 \342\254`:
  # the euro sign loses its middle byte and renders as mojibake. Non-ASCII is left alone ON
  # PURPOSE — a server's message may legitimately be localised, and dropping it would replace the
  # explanation this card exists to surface with exactly that mojibake. A raw C1 byte is not valid
  # UTF-8 anyway, so what survives here is malformed input, not a terminal escape this tool
  # accepted: the render is a single log line under a status, never a terminal control sequence a
  # pager would act on.
  body="$(printf '%s' "$body" | tr '\n\r\t' '   ' | tr -d '\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177' | tr -s ' ')"
  body="${body# }"; body="${body% }"
  if [ -z "$body" ]; then printf 'HTTP %s, and the server sent no body' "$status"; return 0; fi
  if [ "${#body}" -gt "$API_ERR_EXCERPT_MAX" ]; then
    printf 'HTTP %s, server said: %s… [truncated at %s bytes]' \
      "$status" "${body:0:$API_ERR_EXCERPT_MAX}" "$API_ERR_EXCERPT_MAX"
    return 0
  fi
  printf 'HTTP %s, server said: %s' "$status" "$body"
}

# fetch_whole_board — read every card on $BOARD via paged search.json and CENSUS the
# result against the server's own meta.total, so a short read can't silently promote
# from an INCOMPLETE scan (the miss this tool exists to make loud). Prints the deduped
# cards JSON array on stdout. This ports the lib fetch_board_cards backstop (#4338)
# WITHOUT sourcing it — the tool stays a self-contained vendored standalone (like the
# host_ok mirror); it is a co-vendored port, kept honest by promote-pagination-selftest.
# Semantics deliberately mirror the lib EXCEPT the failure policy: the lib returns an rc
# for its caller to interpret, whereas this tool IS the caller and its policy — matching
# its own page-cap check just below — is to DIE on an incomplete read rather than promote
# a partial board.
fetch_whole_board() {
  local cards='[]' page=1 resp data n total='' sum_n=0 read_n last_page=''
  while :; do
    resp="$(api "$API/tasks/search.json?q=board_id=$BOARD&limit=200&page=$page")" \
      || die "board $BOARD read failed — $(resp_detail) — check the token and api_base ($API_SHOWN)"
    if [ "$page" -eq 1 ]; then
      # meta.last_page is a SECONDARY termination signal — the n<200 short-page break is
      # the primary one. It defaults to UNKNOWN (empty), NOT 1: this loop historically
      # paged until a short page, so an ABSENT last_page must fall through to n<200, never
      # break the scan at page 1 on a full 200-row page (that would silently truncate when
      # meta.total is also absent, the very miss #4513 guards). We break on it only when the
      # server actually declares it — the lib's fetch_board_cards now applies the identical
      # guard (card #4623), so this co-vendored copy and the lib agree on last_page semantics.
      last_page="$(printf '%s' "$resp" | jq -r '.meta.last_page // empty' 2>/dev/null)"
      # Usable only as a POSITIVE integer: absent, non-numeric, negative, or <1 all mean
      # "not meaningfully declared" → unknown ('') → fall through to the n<200 break. A
      # last_page of 0 on a full page would otherwise break the scan at page 1 (the same
      # truncation class as an absent value).
      uint_ok "$last_page" || last_page=''
      [ -n "$last_page" ] && [ "$last_page" -lt 1 ] && last_page=''
      total="$(printf '%s' "$resp" | jq -r '.meta.total // empty' 2>/dev/null)"
    fi
    # `.data` must be PRESENT and an ARRAY, on every page. `.data // []` could not tell a
    # page that returned nothing from a page that returned no ANSWER: a 2xx carrying a
    # proxy's JSON error object became `[]`, which is a short page, which ends the loop —
    # so a page-2 fault promoted from a truncated board at rc 0 with nothing on stderr
    # (card#6630; measured here before the fix — page 1 full + page 2 `{"error":…}` gave
    # 200 cards and rc 0). Only page 1 is left to the zero-CARDS die below, which is this
    # tool's own page-1 predicate and DELIBERATELY not the lib's: a mover can treat an
    # empty board as never a working state, a read verb cannot (see fetch_board_cards's
    # parse-site comment in bin/_kb-board-lib.sh). `empty`, not `// []`, for the same
    # reason it is in the lib: the one thing a jq fault can then print is nothing.
    #
    # The `data='[]'` on page 1 is an ACCEPTED collapse, not an oversight: the extraction
    # can now tell an unreadable envelope ('') from a genuinely empty one ('[]'), and this
    # arm deliberately re-merges them so both land on the zero-cards die, whose message
    # names board membership — wrong for a proxy error body, and already wrong for one
    # before this change. Distinguishing them reworks an operator-facing refusal, which is
    # a gated decision; it is tracked on card#6630 in docs/CONSOLIDATION-PLAN.md.
    data="$(printf '%s' "$resp" | jq -c 'if (.data|type) == "array" then .data else empty end')"
    if [ -z "$data" ]; then
      if [ "$page" -eq 1 ]; then
        data='[]'
      else
        die "board $BOARD page $page returned no readable card array (HTTP 2xx) — refusing to promote on an INCOMPLETE board read (a shipped card could be silently missed). Retry."
      fi
    fi
    n="$(printf '%s' "$data" | jq 'length')"
    if [ "$page" -eq 1 ] && [ "${n:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
      die "board $BOARD returned 0 visible cards — the token's user is likely not a member of board $BOARD (or wrong instance). Refusing."
    fi
    # Concatenate via STDIN, not `--argjson` — a board page can exceed MAX_ARG_STRLEN
    # (~128KB), and passing $data/$cards as a CLI arg dies "Argument list too long"
    # (the #3091 argv-overflow class; board 5 was measured crossing the cap at ~98
    # cards, and every board here has grown well past that since).
    cards="$(printf '%s\n%s\n' "$cards" "$data" | jq -c -s 'add')"
    sum_n=$((sum_n + ${n:-0}))
    [ "${n:-0}" -lt 200 ] && break
    [ -n "$last_page" ] && [ "$page" -ge "$last_page" ] && break
    page=$((page + 1))
    [ "$page" -gt "${PROMOTE_PAGE_CAP:-50}" ] && die "promote-released-cards: result set exceeded PROMOTE_PAGE_CAP=${PROMOTE_PAGE_CAP:-50} (page=$page) — refusing to promote on a truncated board read; raise the cap or narrow"
  done
  # Dedup by id — defends against an API that ignores `page=` (same page each loop) or
  # returns overlapping pages; otherwise a card would be PATCHed once per duplicate.
  # (Reorders; harmless — this is a mover keyed on id, not a display surface.)
  cards="$(printf '%s' "$cards" | jq -c 'unique_by(.id)')"
  read_n="$(printf '%s' "$cards" | jq 'length')"
  # Short-read census (#4338), pre-dedup sum vs meta.total tells a REAL undercount from
  # a page-boundary dedup artifact: sum_n < total ⇒ the server delivered fewer rows than
  # it claims exist ⇒ an incomplete scan that would silently leave a shipped card
  # un-promoted ⇒ REFUSE (die), same policy as the page-cap guard above. sum_n >= total
  # with read_n < total ⇒ one card straddled a page boundary and dedup collapsed it ⇒
  # the read is complete ⇒ warn only, promote proceeds.
  if uint_ok "$total" && [ "$total" -gt "${read_n:-0}" ]; then
    if [ "$sum_n" -lt "$total" ]; then
      die "board $BOARD has $total cards but pages delivered only $sum_n ($read_n after dedup) — refusing to promote on an INCOMPLETE board read (a shipped card could be silently missed). Retry; if the board is genuinely larger than the page cap, raise PROMOTE_PAGE_CAP."
    fi
    echo "promote-released-cards: NOTE — read $read_n distinct of $total ($sum_n across pages); a card straddled a page boundary and dedup collapsed it — read complete." >&2
  fi
  printf '%s' "$cards"
}
CARDS="$(fetch_whole_board)"

# --- correlate: a card ships if its dl_number, pr_number OR own id is in the shipped sets
#     AND (when repo qualification is on) the REF legs are this repo's — see REPO QUALIFICATION.
# The classification is done ONCE here, in one pass, and every downstream leg reads `.cls`, so
# the move loop, the reports and the summary cannot disagree about which cards matched.
CORREL="$(printf '%s' "$CARDS" | jq -c --argjson dls "$DLS_J" --argjson prs "$PRS_J" --argjson cids "$CARDS_J" --arg qsrc "$QUALIFY_SRC" --arg ptag "$PROGRAM_TAG" '
  # norm reads a card ref (dl_number, pr_number or id) and answers the bare-integer,
  # zero-stripped form numlist produces for the shipped side, so "093"/93 correlate — or ""
  # for a value that is not ONE integer, which correlates to nothing and leaves the card alone.
  #
  # THE ACCEPT SET IS THE RULE THE BOARD ITSELF APPLIES (kanban DL-251: `^\D*(\d+)\D*$`), and
  # the "" arm is the whole fix (card#7587). Stripping every non-digit CONCATENATES the runs
  # of a multi-run value — "1.5" -> 15, "2026-08-23" -> 20260823, "PR 12 of 34" -> 1234 — so a
  # card holding "1.5" correlated against shipped PR 15, a real but UNRELATED pull request, and
  # this sweep PATCHed it to the released stage. The provenance of that stamp is arbitrary
  # (board UI, another tool, a human; `kbcard` refuses to MINT one, one writer of many), so the
  # reader is where it has to be answered. Refusing is the recoverable direction: an
  # un-promoted card is moved by hand, a card moved onto an unrelated PR is a durable event in
  # the changelog of the board that looks legitimate afterwards.
  #
  # ONE def FOR ALL THREE REF FIELDS, deliberately — re-splitting it per field is how they
  # start stripping differently. A card `id` is a board integer and an id can only ever be one
  # run, so the guard is a no-op there; it costs nothing and keeps the single source.
  #
  # `\A`/`\z`, NOT `^`/`$`: the jq regex engine is Oniguruma, where `^`/`$` are LINE anchors
  # unless the syntax in force sets SINGLELINE — under one that does not, a stored "1\n5" would
  # satisfy an `^…$` test on its first line and normalize to 15, i.e. the exact defect, straight
  # through the guard. ⚠ HONEST SCOPE, so this is not over-cited: MEASURED on jq 1.7 the two
  # spellings answer identically, and no jq that separates them has been exercised here, so the
  # selftest pins the embedded-newline VALUE but CANNOT discriminate the two spellings (an
  # `^…$` mutant of this line is green there). It is written this way because this is a vendored
  # standalone that runs on whatever jq a consumer has, and the absolute anchors do not depend
  # on which regex options that jq was built with.
  # `[^0-9]` is a NEGATED set of ASCII bytes, so no locale collation widens it.
  #
  # ⛔ NO APOSTROPHE ANYWHERE IN THIS jq PROGRAM — the CORREL= one, whose bounds are the
  # opening single quote on the `CORREL="$(printf ... | jq -c ...` line above and its close at
  # the end of this block. (The variable was named PLAN= when this warning was written. PLAN=
  # is now a DIFFERENT, one-line jq program further down, so naming it here sent the next
  # author to the wrong bounds — and the bounds are the whole content of the warning.)
  # These are jq comments inside a SHELL single-quoted string: an apostrophe ends
  # that string, and everything after it is re-parsed as shell — which fails at RUNTIME, not
  # at shellcheck time. Orientation for the next author, not the guard: every selftest that
  # drives this tool as a process reds immediately, because no run survives it.
  def norm: (. // "")|tostring|if test("\\A[^0-9]*[0-9]+[^0-9]*\\z") then gsub("[^0-9]";"")|sub("^0+(?=.)";"") else "" end;

  # ── source derivation: a jq mirror of the kanban server source normalizer (card#8421) ──
  # OWNER OF THE RULE, and it is NOT in the kanban repo — a locator naming it there was read
  # live and is false. Two surfaces, both confirmed present:
  #   * the PROSE contract — DL-SOURCE-CORRELATION.md sections 1 and 2, published by the coord
  #     plugin at .claude/plugins/marketplaces/agent-board-framework/plugins/coord/docs/. This
  #     is the surface a CONSUMER of this vendored script can actually reach, and it is the
  #     only DECLARE surface this mirror has, so it has to resolve.
  #   * the CODE source of truth it mirrors — app/Services/ExternalReferenceNormalizer.php in
  #     the kanban-board repo.
  # The rule is NOT restated here — read it there.
  # This is a FOURTH runtime expressing it (server PHP, bridge PHP, the python client
  # kanban_common._derive_card_source, and this jq), for the same reason host_ok is duplicated
  # in this file: the script is VENDORED STANDALONE into consumer repos and may not source a
  # lib nor shell out to python. The copy is bound BEHAVIOURALLY, not by this comment —
  # tests/promote-source-qualify-selftest.sh drives it over a corpus covering every field of
  # the preference order, the trailing-segment requirement and the .git trim.
  #
  # ⛔ WHY NOT ASK THE SERVER, which already owns this rule — considered, and rejected on what
  # the ENDPOINT can answer, not on effort. `GET /boards/{b}/tasks/by-ref.json?system=dl&ref=N
  # &source=<repo>` (the query bin/adopt-to-dl step-5-verifies with) applies the qualification
  # server-side, so a 4th runtime looks avoidable. Read live, its filter is
  # `->where(source, $source)` — a STRICT equality, so a card whose source is another repo, and
  # a card whose source is NULL, are both simply ABSENT from the answer, indistinguishable from
  # "no card carries this ref". Those two rows ARE the whole report here: the ⊘ lines are the only
  # proof the guard fired, and the unsourced one is the only thing that tells an operator to
  # STAMP a card rather than mint one. The endpoint that answers the qualification cannot
  # produce the report the qualification exists to make. Two lesser reasons, both measured:
  # `external_references` is whenLoaded-gated on TaskResource (the "externalReferences" relation) and
  # `tasks/search.json` eager-loads only `lastStageMoveChangelog`, so the paged board read this
  # tool already makes carries no `source` at all — adopting by-ref means one request per
  # (system, ref) in place of one paginated scan, and the scan is also where `workflow_stage_id`
  # (idempotence, --shipped-stages) comes from; and the `--cards` leg correlates on the OWN ID of a
  # card, which is not an external-reference system, so it has no by-ref query in the first
  # place. Recorded in docs/CONSOLIDATION-PLAN.md as well, so the decision is not only a comment.
  #
  # canonicalizeSource: trim, then empty means null, then cap 255, then lowercase.
  # Deliberately NO .git trim and NO shape check here — both belong to repo_from_gh_url,
  # upstream, and only for URL values.
  #
  # WHAT BINDS THE TWO SIDES OF THE ONE COMPARISON THIS SCRIPT MAKES — stated exactly, because
  # the claim this comment used to make ("BOTH sides run through THIS def") is FALSE: the CONFIG
  # side is canonicalized in SHELL, above, by `src_canon` (sed trim, 255-cap, LC_ALL=C tr — one
  # function, both of its callers), and never reaches this def. What makes the two agree is
  # narrower, and it is ENFORCED rather than assumed: the config side must first pass
  # `src_charset_ok` and the shape `case`, which admit only ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-`
  # and the one `/` — and over exactly that set `src_canon` and this def are identical (nothing
  # to trim, an ASCII-only fold, the same cap).
  #
  # KNOWN DIVERGENCES FROM THE PHP, all of them on the CARD side (the config side cannot carry
  # any of them, per the charset above):
  #   * trim(): PHP strips NUL and does NOT strip \f; Oniguruma [[:space:]] does the opposite.
  #     \f is the PROMOTING direction — this def trims it, the server does not, so a card source
  #     of "acme/widget\f" matches here and would not there. NUL is the refusing direction.
  #     Neither byte can appear in a GitHub owner/repo, nor in a URL-path capture.
  #   * mb_strtolower vs ascii_downcase: unreachable for this comparison in either direction —
  #     a value needing a non-ASCII fold cannot equal an ASCII-only config value either way.
  #   * `tostring` on a non-string used to be a third, and the WORST, because it ran toward
  #     promoting on ordinary board data rather than on a control byte. It is FIXED below:
  #     repo_from_gh_url now type-guards exactly as the server does with `is_string`.
  def canon_source:
    if . == null then null
    else (tostring | sub("^[[:space:]]+";"") | sub("[[:space:]]+$";"")) as $t
      | if $t == "" then null else ($t[0:255] | ascii_downcase) end
    end;
  # repoFromGitHubUrl: a github.com URL yields a source ONLY when a
  # /{pull|issues|commit|tree|blob}/ segment follows the repo — a BARE github.com/<owner>/<repo>
  # yields null, and that IS the rule, not an oversight. Non-greedy repo part; a trailing .git
  # is trimmed HERE and only here. No "g" flag, so first match only, like preg_match.
  #
  # ⛔ NON-STRINGS DERIVE NOTHING, and the type test is the whole of it. The server reads these
  # fields behind `is_string` (sourceFor, and `is_string($externalLink)` for the top-level one),
  # so a payload value that is an OBJECT or an ARRAY yields null there. This def used to
  # `tostring` first — which renders such a value as its JSON TEXT, so a `{"u":"https://github.
  # com/acme/widget/pull/1"}` matched the capture here and yielded a source the server says does
  # not exist. That disagreement ran toward PROMOTING, which is the one direction this guard
  # exists to close. The arm also keeps `capture` off a non-string, which is a jq runtime error.
  def repo_from_gh_url:
    if type != "string" then null
    else (capture("github[.]com/(?<r>[^/]+/[^/]+?)([.]git)?/(pull|issues|commit|tree|blob)/"; "i")).r // null
    end;
  # by-ref-source-rule: HOME — the code-side full statement, for a copy vendored standalone with
  # no docs/ beside it. tests/by-ref-source-claim-selftest.sh holds the field order stated here
  # against the def below, which is the one executable answer, and holds the other declared HOMEs
  # against the same def.
  # sourceFor field preference: payload.repo — ONLY when it is a string containing "/", and it
  # then WINS outright, with no fall-through on a null canon — then payload.pr_url,
  # payload.issue_url, payload.html_url, and finally the top-level external_link.
  def derive_source:
    . as $c | ($c.payload // {}) as $p
    | if (($p.repo | type) == "string") and ($p.repo | test("/"))
      then ($p.repo | canon_source)
      else ( [ $p.pr_url, $p.issue_url, $p.html_url, $c.external_link ]
             | map(repo_from_gh_url) | map(select(. != null)) | .[0] ) as $u
        | if $u == null then null else ($u | canon_source) end
      end;
  [ .[]
    | . as $c
    | ($c.payload.dl_number | norm) as $cdl
    | ($c.payload.pr_number | norm) as $cpr
    | ($c.id | norm) as $cid
    | (($cdl != "") and ($dls|index($cdl) != null)) as $byDl
    | (($cpr != "") and ($prs|index($cpr) != null)) as $byPr
    | (($cid != "") and ($cids|index($cid) != null)) as $byId
    | select($byDl or $byPr or $byId)
    | ($c | derive_source) as $csrc
    # THE PROGRAM-PARENT PREDICATE, read HERE because this is where the row still is (card#10068).
    # The board resource this scan already fetched projects `tags` on every row, so the predicate
    # costs ZERO extra requests — but this projection used to drop the field, so the move loop
    # below could not see a tag the tool had already been told. That is the whole of what made the
    # withhold look like new I/O.
    # EXACT string match, deliberately: see the header. `select(type == "string")` keeps a
    # non-string member out of the compare rather than letting it decide it, and the `[…[]?]`
    # form answers `false` for a `tags` that is absent or not a list — the degrade-toward-promoting
    # direction the header names as a blind spot rather than hides.
    | (([($c.tags // [])[]? | select(type == "string")] | index($ptag)) != null) as $prog
    # Display precedence keeps DL first so a run with no --cards renders exactly as before.
    | { id: $c.id, stage: ($c.workflow_stage_id|tostring), program: $prog,
        ref: (if $byDl then "DL-\($cdl)" elif $byId then "card#\($cid)" else "#\($cpr)" end),
        dl: (if $byDl then $cdl else null end),
        pr: (if $byPr then $cpr else null end),
        card: (if $byId then $cid else null end),
        src: $csrc,
        # match     — promote it: qualification is OFF, the card is THIS repo, or the caller
        #             named its ID (a card id is not a repo-scoped counter — see the header).
        # foreign   — ref-matched, but the card belongs to a DIFFERENT repo. The card#8421 defect.
        # unsourced — ref-matched, but NO source is derivable, so it cannot be attributed.
        cls: (if   $qsrc == ""    then "match"
              elif $byId          then "match"
              elif $csrc == null  then "unsourced"
              elif $csrc == $qsrc then "match"
              else                     "foreign" end),
        # An id-matched card the qualification WOULD have rejected. Promoted, by the --cards
        # exemption — but the exemption is reported rather than silent, because a promote whose
        # only justification is an explicit id is a thing an operator should see in the log.
        idxrepo: ($byId and $qsrc != "" and ($csrc == null or $csrc != $qsrc)) } ]')"
PLAN="$(printf '%s' "$CORREL" | jq -c '[.[]|select(.cls=="match")]')"

# A shipped DL or CARD ID with no matching card is the high-value miss (a decision whose
# card is unstamped/absent, or an id naming no card on this board) — warn on it. Under repo
# qualification the population it is computed over is the PLAN — the cards this repo may move —
# LESS the refs that matched an unsourced card, which get their own line below. So a DL whose
# only match was another repo's card IS reported here as no-card, and correctly: no card OF THIS
# REPO carries it. The ⊘ line above names the card that did, so the two reports together say
# what happened; neither on its own would. Unmatched
# PRs are NOT reported: a PR is only a correlation fallback and routinely has no card (dep
# bumps, release commits), so warning on them is cry-wolf noise.
#
# THE NO-CARD REPORT IS EXACTLY TWO LINES, and which refs go on which is the contract
# release-pr-body's `card_coverage_report` is written against — it greps BOTH, by their
# leading phrases. A THIRD kind may not be added here without teaching that function to read
# it, or the body silently half-reads a report it believes is whole.
#   line 1, "matched NO card"  — a shipped ref no card of this repo carries. `foreign` refs
#     belong here and stay here: "no card OF THIS REPO carries it" is exactly true of them, and
#     the ⊘ line above already names the other repo's card that did, so the two reports together
#     say what happened. The remedy is to create or correct a card.
#   line 2, "matched ONLY an unsourced card" — a shipped ref whose ONLY correlation was a card
#     this run could not attribute. That card EXISTS and carries the ref; what it lacks is a
#     by-ref source. Folding it into line 1 made release-pr-body tell an author to CREATE a card
#     that was already on the board, and the two remedies are opposites — that is why the split
#     is a second line rather than more prose on the first. Where a ref matched an unsourced
#     card AND a foreign one, the unsourced report wins: it is the one naming a card that may
#     well be this repo's, and it is the actionable half. ⚠ THE PRECEDENCE IS NAMED IN THE LINE
#     ITSELF, because the word ONLY is not true of that ref on its own — it matched a foreign
#     card too. The anchor phrase is deliberately left byte-identical: `bin/release-pr-body`
#     greps it, `command -v promote-released-cards` resolves that reader against whatever copy
#     is on PATH, and the two are separately vendorable (INSTALL.md §6a ships this one as a
#     SHA-pinned composite action), so a reworded anchor is a silently half-read report across
#     any version skew. The qualifier goes AFTER the phrase and carries no colon, per the
#     colon rule below.
# Unmatched PRs are on neither line (see above). With --cards absent the card leg is empty, and
# under `source: "*"` nothing can classify as unsourced — so line 2 is absent and line 1 is
# byte-identical to the DL-only report this tool has always printed.
MATCHED_DLS="$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -c '[.[].dl|select(.!=null)]|unique')"
MATCHED_CARDS="$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -c '[.[].card|select(.!=null)]|unique')"
# Only DLs can land here. A `--cards` id-matched row is classified "match" by the exemption arm
# in the correlation above, so no card ID ever carries cls=="unsourced".
UNSOURCED_DLS="$(printf '%s' "$CORREL" | jq -c '[.[]|select(.cls=="unsourced")|.dl|select(.!=null)]|unique')"
MISSING="$(jq -c -n --argjson sd "$DLS_J" --argjson md "$MATCHED_DLS" --argjson ud "$UNSOURCED_DLS" \
                    --argjson sc "$CARDS_J" --argjson mc "$MATCHED_CARDS" '
  [ $sd[] | select(. as $x | (($md|index($x))|not) and (($ud|index($x))|not)) | "DL-\(.)" ]
  + [ $sc[] | select(. as $x | ($mc|index($x))|not) | "card#\(.)" ]')"
STRANDED_REFS="$(jq -c -n --argjson ud "$UNSOURCED_DLS" --argjson md "$MATCHED_DLS" '
  [ $ud[] | select(. as $x | ($md|index($x))|not) | "DL-\(.)" ]')"

# --- repo-qualification report (card#8421) -----------------------------------
# The two rejected classes are NAMED, one line per card, BEFORE any write — never folded into
# a count and never silently dropped. `foreign` is the defect this card closes and its lines
# are the proof the guard fired; `unsourced` is a card this run genuinely cannot attribute, and
# it is the one an operator must act on (stamp it, or promote it by hand).
#
# Both are WARN-ONLY and neither touches the exit code, for the same reason the stage guard's
# skips do not: they are a PRE-EXISTING BOARD CONDITION, not a failure of this run, and a red
# clearable only by editing board data is a red that gets ignored. Loudness comes from the
# register instead — stderr, plus $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY under Actions, because a stderr line in
# a GREEN job is invisible in a collapsed log.
foreign_n=0; unsourced_n=0; STRANDED=""; FOREIGN=""
if [ -n "$QUALIFY_SRC" ]; then
  foreign_n="$(printf '%s' "$CORREL" | jq '[.[]|select(.cls=="foreign")]|length')"
  unsourced_n="$(printf '%s' "$CORREL" | jq '[.[]|select(.cls=="unsourced")]|length')"
  # Rendered BY jq, not assembled in the shell: every value interpolated here is BOARD DATA,
  # and a card ref or source must never be re-parsed by a shell on its way to the log.
  # Captured rather than printed straight through, so the SAME text reaches stderr and the step
  # summary — two renderings of one finding is two things that can disagree.
  FOREIGN="$(printf '%s' "$CORREL" | jq -r --arg q "$QUALIFY_SRC" '.[] | select(.cls=="foreign")
    | "  ⊘ \(.ref) (#\(.id)): card source \"\(.src)\" is NOT \"\($q)\" — this card belongs to a DIFFERENT repo and matched only by a colliding ref number; not promoted"')"
  # A plain `if`, never `[ -n "$X" ] && printf …`: that trailing-test form is what leaked a false
  # status out of a sibling release bin once already (card#5874), and it is one edit away from
  # becoming a block's last statement again.
  if [ -n "$FOREIGN" ]; then printf '%s\n' "$FOREIGN" >&2; fi
  # THE KEY LIST IN THE LINE BELOW IS A COPY ON PURPOSE. It is RENDERED to an operator, who cannot
  # follow a pointer out of a log line, so the five keys are spelled where they are read. What
  # keeps it from becoming a fifth unpinned restatement is the trailing marker: it declares this a
  # statement of the field set, and tests/by-ref-source-claim-selftest.sh holds it to derive_source
  # above — a key added to that def and not to this line reds.
  STRANDED="$(printf '%s' "$CORREL" | jq -r --arg q "$QUALIFY_SRC" '.[] | select(.cls=="unsourced")
    | "  ⊘ \(.ref) (#\(.id)): card has NO by-ref source (no payload.repo / pr_url / issue_url / html_url / external_link) — cannot be attributed to \"\($q)\"; not promoted. Stamp a repo-qualified payload.pr_url (kbcard patch --pr-url) and re-run, or promote by hand."')"  # by-ref-source-rule: HOME
  if [ -n "$STRANDED" ]; then printf '%s\n' "$STRANDED" >&2; fi
  # The --cards exemption, made legible. These cards ARE promoted; the line exists so that
  # "an id outranks the repo guard" is something an operator reads in the log rather than
  # something they have to re-derive from this file.
  printf '%s' "$CORREL" | jq -r --arg q "$QUALIFY_SRC" '.[] | select(.idxrepo)
    | "  ⚠ \(.ref) (#\(.id)): promoted by EXPLICIT card id, though its source \(.src|tostring) is not \"\($q)\" — a card id is not a repo-scoped counter, so --cards is exempt from repo qualification"' >&2
  # BOTH rejected classes reach the step summary, for the one stated reason: they are WARN-only,
  # so they land in a GREEN job, where a stderr line is invisible in a collapsed log. That
  # argument never distinguished the two — `foreign` lines are the only proof the guard fired at
  # all, which is the half an operator auditing a `.promote.source` change goes looking for.
  if [ -n "$FOREIGN" ] && [ -n "${GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY:-}" ]; then
    { echo "### promote-released-cards — $foreign_n ref-matched card(s) belong to ANOTHER repo"
      echo ""
      echo "Not promoted. This board is repo-qualified (\`.promote.source\` = \`$QUALIFY_SRC\`) and these cards resolve to a different \`owner/repo\`, so they matched only by a colliding ref number."
      echo ""
      printf '```\n%s\n```\n' "$FOREIGN"
    } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY" 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
  if [ -n "$STRANDED" ] && [ -n "${GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY:-}" ]; then
    { echo "### promote-released-cards — $unsourced_n ref-matched card(s) had NO by-ref source"
      echo ""
      echo "Not promoted. This board is repo-qualified (\`.promote.source\` = \`$QUALIFY_SRC\`) and these cards carry nothing to derive \`owner/repo\` from, so this run cannot tell whose they are."
      echo ""
      printf '```\n%s\n```\n' "$STRANDED"
    } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY" 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
fi

# --- the read-back: what the board HOLDS after the write ------------------------
# ⛔ THE 2xx IS NOT THE MOVE. A status is the server saying it ACCEPTED the request; only a read
# says what it now holds. Measured on this fleet 2026-05-22: 28 of 28 cards PATCHed, every call
# 2xx, `updated_at` bumped on every card, `workflow_stage_id` unchanged on every card — and the
# run printed `✓ … moved` 28 times and exited 0, so the release read as promoted and nobody
# looked again. That is the card#8556 class, one tool over: a success built out of what was SENT.
#
# ⚠ ONE read serves BOTH the stage ruling and the owner-tag clear below, because it is literally
# the same request — the clear already re-read the card after the move, so a second GET here
# would be two requests asking one question, and two answers that can disagree.

# card_stage <GET body>: the card's workflow_stage_id as TEXT, or NOTHING when none can be read
# out of the body. A string and a number both answer (the board stores the integer this tool
# sent, and a JSON string spelling of it is the same stage) — `tostring` is what makes the two
# comparable, and it is the same normalisation kbcard's `_kbc_confirm_stage` applies to its own
# echo, which § 6 of tests/mirror-pair-parity-selftest.sh pins.
#
# ⚠ ABSENT AND UNREADABLE DELIBERATELY COLLAPSE to the same empty answer, which is the one place
# in this tool where that collapse is not the read-outcome-collapse defect: the caller's arm for
# an empty answer makes NO CLAIM in either direction — it reports UNVERIFIED — so the two states
# it merges are being treated as the one thing they genuinely share, "nothing was measured".
#
# ⚠ THE MULTI-LINE REFUSAL IS STATED AT ITS ACTUAL PREDICATE, WHICH IS NARROWER THAN "one JSON
# text". `jq -r` over a body carrying several JSON texts emits one line per SURVIVING projection,
# so `*$'\n'*` refuses a body that yields TWO OR MORE STAGES — not ONE card, so it is refused
# rather than having its first line read as the card's stage. A multi-text body in which only one
# text survives both `select`s yields ONE line and is read: measured, `{"data":{"id":1}}` followed
# by `{"data":{"workflow_stage_id":85}}` prints `85`. Left standing rather than narrowed, because
# narrowing it changes what this tool ACCEPTS — its own decision to ask for — and this API is not
# known to produce a multi-text body. What was wrong was the CLAIM, and the claim is what moved.
card_stage() {
  local s
  s="$(jq -r '.data | select(type == "object") | .workflow_stage_id | select(type == "number" or type == "string") | tostring' <<<"$1" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  case "$s" in *$'\n'*) return 0 ;; esac
  printf '%s' "$s"
}

# stage_verdict <read rc> <stage read back, EMPTY when none could be read> <stage asked for>:
# the write-outcome ruling for a stage PATCH that already answered SUCCESS, as one word —
# `applied`, `not-applied` or `unverified`. It rules; it says nothing, because the message is the
# caller's (this file names the card and the ref; the rule does not know them).
#
# MIRRORS `_kbc_confirm_stage` in bin/kbcard (card#8556) and is driven beside it over one corpus
# by tests/mirror-pair-parity-selftest.sh § 6, WITH the one declared divergence asserted as a
# value in both directions: a card whose stage cannot be read is `unverified` HERE and HARD
# FAILURE there. The two are ruling on different subjects, which is why neither is wrong —
# kbcard rules on the server's own write ECHO, whose readability `_kbc_write_echo` has already
# established (an unreadable echo is its rc 3, one layer up), so a missing stage in a body it has
# accepted really is a disagreement; this rules on an INDEPENDENT GET that nothing upstream has
# vouched for, so "no stage could be read" is nothing measured, and nothing measured may not be
# reported as a measurement.
stage_verdict() {
  [ "$1" -eq 0 ] || { printf 'unverified'; return 0; }
  [ -n "$2" ]    || { printf 'unverified'; return 0; }
  [ "$2" = "$3" ] && printf 'applied' || printf 'not-applied'
}

# --- the seat owner tag on a card this run released -----------------------------
# MIRRORS the lib's clear — kb_card_tags, kb_owner_strip and `kb_owner_tag_write clear` in
# _kb-board-lib.sh — because this script is vendored standalone and must not source that lib.
# tests/mirror-pair-parity-selftest.sh drives owner_card_tags, owner_strip and owner_list beside
# their lib twins.
# ⚠ THE MIRROR IS NOT EXACT AS OF card#9938, and the divergence is on this file's side: the
# report below is built from a READ-BACK, while `kb_owner_tag_write` still decides its
# `stamped` / `removed owner tag(s)` line from `case "$http" in 2*)`. The parity test pins the
# tag RULES (which tags are owner tags, what a strip leaves), not the clear's control flow, so
# nothing mechanical holds these two together on this property — the same defect is live in the
# lib, is NAMED in README § The seat owner tag and in the changelog entry, and needs its own
# change: the lib function is shared with kbcard and board-card-start.
# owner_card_tags <GET body>: the card's tag list as compact JSON, or NOTHING when none can be read.
owner_card_tags() { jq -c '.data | select(type == "object") | (if has("tags") and .tags != null then .tags else [] end) | select(type == "array")' <<<"$1" 2>/dev/null || true; }
# owner_strip <tags>: the list without its owner tags, or NOTHING when it carries none (no write).
owner_strip() { jq -c 'def is_owner: type == "string" and startswith("owner:"); if any(.[]; is_owner) then map(select(is_owner | not)) else empty end' <<<"$1" 2>/dev/null || true; }
# owner_list <tags>: the owner tags the list carries, comma-joined (for the report line).
owner_list() { jq -r 'def is_owner: type == "string" and startswith("owner:"); [.[] | select(is_owner)] | join(", ")' <<<"$1" 2>/dev/null || true; }

# owner_clear <card id> <ref> <card read after the move>: PATCH `{tags}` alone without the card's
# owner tags, then READ THE CARD BACK and rule on what it now carries. Always returns 0 — the
# move's report, the counts and the exit code are the move's, never this write's.
#
# ⛔ WHY ITS OUTCOME IS STILL NOT AN EXIT CODE, when the move's now is. That separation is a
# SHIPPED, DOCUMENTED property of this tool (this file's header; README § The seat owner tag: "the
# move's outcome and the command's exit code do not change"), and a stale owner tag is not a wrong
# release record — the card IS released, and the tag is a claim nobody is acting on. What card#9938
# fixes here is the REPORT: the `✓ removed owner tag(s)` line was built out of the PATCH's status,
# so a 2xx that changed nothing printed the same success as one that worked. It is now built out
# of a read, in the SAME vocabulary the move uses, and the read-back is what it quotes.
#
# ⚠ IT NO LONGER ISSUES THE FIRST READ, and it no longer has an arm for that read FAILING. The
# caller reads the card once after the move — the stage ruling and this clear were asking one
# question of one card — and only reaches here on the arm where that read SUCCEEDED and said the
# card moved. A `the card's tags could not be read` arm here would be defending against a state
# its only caller cannot produce; the read-failure case is reported by the move, as UNVERIFIED,
# where the card whose tags these are is not even known to have moved.
owner_clear() {
  local id="$1" ref="$2" resp="$3" tags kept gone rc=0 back left
  tags="$(owner_card_tags "$resp")"
  if [ -z "$tags" ]; then
    echo "  ⚠ $ref (#$id): owner tags NOT cleared — the card read succeeded but no tag list could be read out of it, and the board replaces the tag list wholesale, so none is sent; the card was moved" >&2
    return 0
  fi
  kept="$(owner_strip "$tags")"
  [ -n "$kept" ] || return 0
  gone="$(owner_list "$tags")"
  api -X PATCH -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "$(jq -nc --argjson t "$kept" '{tags: $t}')" "$API/tasks/$id.json" >/dev/null || rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" = "$API_RC_REFUSED" ]; then
    echo "  ⚠ $ref (#$id): owner tags NOT cleared — $(resp_detail); the card was moved (read back above) and its tags are unchanged" >&2
    return 0
  fi
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    echo "  ⚠ $ref (#$id): owner tag clear NOT CONFIRMED — $(resp_detail); the card was moved (read back above), and the tag request did not complete, so whether it was written is UNKNOWN" >&2
    return 0
  fi
  # 2xx. NOW READ WHAT THE BOARD HOLDS — the status said the server accepted the write, which is
  # not the same sentence as "the tags are gone".
  rc=0
  back="$(api "$API/tasks/$id.json")" || rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    echo "  ⚠ $ref (#$id): owner tag clear UNVERIFIED — the write was SENT and answered success, but the card could NOT be re-read ($(resp_detail)), so nothing here says the tag(s) $gone are gone and nothing says they are not" >&2
    return 0
  fi
  back="$(owner_card_tags "$back")"
  if [ -z "$back" ]; then
    echo "  ⚠ $ref (#$id): owner tag clear UNVERIFIED — the write was SENT and answered success, but no tag list could be read out of the card afterwards, so the tag(s) $gone are UNMEASURED" >&2
    return 0
  fi
  left="$(owner_list "$back")"
  if [ -n "$left" ]; then
    echo "  ✗ $ref (#$id): owner tags NOT cleared — the PATCH answered success and the card STILL carries $left. The status is not the write; the read-back is." >&2
    return 0
  fi
  echo "  ✓ $ref (#$id): removed owner tag(s) $gone — read back: the card now carries no owner tag, so a released card holds nobody's claim"
  return 0
}

# --- the COMPLETENESS GATE (opt-in: --require-complete) ----------------------
# ONE consultation for the whole matched set, BEFORE any write, so the gate's own failure cannot
# leave a half-promoted release: every card gets a verdict or none does, and "none" is
# UNMEASURED, which refuses. ⛔ NO PREDICATE LIVES HERE — `card-completeness` owns what
# "complete" means; this block owns only what to do with its answer.
COMPLETE_VERDICTS=""; gate_head=""; gate_head_src=""; gate_integration=""; gate_integration_src=""
if [ "$REQUIRE_COMPLETE" = 1 ]; then
  # A sibling of THIS file, resolved from the script's own path rather than $PWD: it is vendored
  # into a consumer's bin/ and run from the repo root.
  [ -n "$COMPLETENESS" ] || COMPLETENESS="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/card-completeness"
  # ⛔ RESOLVED HERE, ABOVE EVERY BRANCH, BECAUSE THE STATE LINE AT THE END REPORTS THEM AND A
  # RUN THAT ASKED NOTHING STILL HAS TO SAY WHAT IT WOULD HAVE ASKED ABOUT. These two values —
  # not the oracle's path — are what DEFINES the population the gate measured, and each resolves
  # from two channels that nothing else in the output tells apart:
  #   * the release head. The verdict is ABOUT a commit, as a full sha, because the oracle asks
  #     GitHub and a local ref name means nothing there; an unresolvable head reaches it EMPTY
  #     and it refuses by name. With no --head that commit is WHATEVER THE CWD IS CHECKED OUT AT
  #     — run from an integration-branch checkout the window is `<dev>...<dev>`, i.e. empty, and
  #     clause (b) has nothing to find while the run reports `0 completeness-refused` at rc 0.
  #   * the integration branch, `.dev_branch`, defaulted exactly as release-pr-body defaults the
  #     same key so one key has one reading. That key lives in a PR-EDITABLE file and is NOT one
  #     of the correlation keys `bin/release-artifacts-check` guards, so a PR setting it to the
  #     release branch makes clause (b)'s window permanently empty — again at rc 0.
  # Neither failure has a symptom. Printing the pair WITH ITS CHANNEL is what makes the gate's
  # population falsifiable from the log, which is the rule `docs/INSTALL.md` §4 already states
  # for this tool's other two-source resolution (the base) and which this gate was missing.
  gate_head="$(git rev-parse --verify --quiet "${HEAD_REF:-HEAD}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  # ⚠ NO APOSTROPHE IN THIS DEFAULT. A `'` inside `${x:-…}` opens a single quote even within the
  # surrounding double quotes, and bash then reports the unterminated string as a syntax error
  # 40 lines further down — measured here, on the first spelling of this line.
  gate_head_src="${HEAD_SRC:-the working tree HEAD (no --head passed)}"
  gate_integration="$(cfg '.dev_branch')"
  if [ -n "$gate_integration" ]; then gate_integration_src="$CONFIG .dev_branch"
  else                                gate_integration="dev"; gate_integration_src="the built-in default"; fi
  gate_ids="$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -r '[.[].id] | join(",")')"
  if [ -z "$gate_ids" ]; then
    :   # nothing matched, so there is nothing to ask about
  elif [ ! -x "$COMPLETENESS" ]; then
    # NOT a fallback to "no gate": an unanswered question does not write a terminal stage.
    COMPLETE_VERDICTS="$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -r --arg p "$COMPLETENESS" \
      '.[].id | "\(.)\tUNMEASURED\tthe completeness oracle is not executable at \($p) (pass --completeness PATH, or drop --require-complete to promote without it)"')"
  else
    gate_rc=0
    COMPLETE_VERDICTS="$("$COMPLETENESS" --release-head "$gate_head" --integration "$gate_integration" --cards "$gate_ids")" || gate_rc=$?
    # 0 all complete, 5 at least one incomplete, 6 unmeasured — each carries one verdict line per
    # card. Anything else is the oracle REFUSING to run (its reason is on stderr, with no
    # verdicts), so every card is synthesised UNMEASURED rather than left to read as "no answer".
    case "$gate_rc" in
      0|5|6) ;;
      *) COMPLETE_VERDICTS="$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -r --arg p "$COMPLETENESS" --arg rc "$gate_rc" \
           '.[].id | "\(.)\tUNMEASURED\tthe completeness oracle (\($p)) refused to run, exit \($rc) — its reason is on stderr above"')" ;;
    esac
  fi
fi

# complete_verdict <card-id> — "<verdict>\t<detail>". An id with NO line is UNMEASURED: a missing
# answer and a clean answer must never read the same.
complete_verdict() {
  printf '%s\n' "$COMPLETE_VERDICTS" | awk -F'\t' -v id="$1" '
    $1 == id { print $2 "\t" $3; found = 1; exit }
    END { if (!found) print "UNMEASURED\tthe completeness oracle returned no verdict for this card" }'
}

# --- move --------------------------------------------------------------------
moved=0; skipped=0; failed=0; guarded=0; unverified=0; not_applied=0; incomplete=0; program_held=0
while IFS= read -r row; do
  [ -n "$row" ] || continue
  id="$(printf '%s' "$row" | jq -r '.id')"
  cur="$(printf '%s' "$row" | jq -r '.stage')"
  ref="$(printf '%s' "$row" | jq -r '.ref')"
  if [ "$cur" = "$STAGE" ]; then echo "  = $ref (#$id): already released"; skipped=$((skipped + 1)); continue; fi
  # Source-stage guard (opt-in via --shipped-stages). Promotion means Shipped→Released, so a
  # matched card NOT in a Shipped-class source stage is skipped, not moved — promotion never
  # resurrects a declined/backlog card carrying a stale or recycled DL/PR stamp. Evaluated
  # AFTER the already-released check so an idempotent re-run of a genuinely-released card still
  # reads as "already released" (its now-Released stage is not a Shipped-class one).
  if [ -n "$SHIPPED_STAGES" ] && ! in_shipped_stage "$cur"; then
    echo "  ⊘ $ref (#$id): current stage $cur not in a Shipped-class source stage — skipped; promotion never resurrects declined/backlog cards" >&2
    guarded=$((guarded + 1)); continue
  fi
  # COMPLETENESS GATE (opt-in). AFTER the already-released and source-stage checks, so an
  # idempotent re-run and a stage-guarded card still read as what they are; BEFORE the dry-run
  # branch, so a preview shows the refusal the real run would make.
  if [ "$REQUIRE_COMPLETE" = 1 ]; then
    IFS=$'\t' read -r verdict detail < <(complete_verdict "$id")
    case "$verdict" in
      COMPLETE) ;;
      INCOMPLETE)
        echo "  ⊘ $ref (#$id): INCOMPLETE — PR(s) this release does not contain still carry this card's work: $detail. REFUSED: promoting it would make a partial implementation's stage terminal." >&2
        incomplete=$((incomplete + 1)); continue ;;
      *)
        echo "  ⊘ $ref (#$id): completeness UNMEASURED — $detail. REFUSED: 'no unreleased PR names this card' and 'the PRs could not be read' are the same silence, and only one of them licenses a terminal stage." >&2
        incomplete=$((incomplete + 1)); continue ;;
    esac
  fi
  # PROGRAM-PARENT WITHHOLD (card#10068) — unconditional, non-fatal. Why the tag, why exact, why a
  # withhold rather than a refusal: the header block, which --help prints. Not restated here.
  #
  # ⛔ WHY IT IS LAST, IMMEDIATELY BEFORE THE WRITE — the position IS the rc contract. Every check
  # above (already-released, --shipped-stages, --require-complete) runs on a parent exactly as it
  # did before the withhold existed, reports it exactly as it did, and reaches its rc exactly as it
  # did. The tag only matters for a card that would otherwise MOVE, so it is consulted only there,
  # and the one fact it changes is that the card is not PATCHed: `program_held` stands in for the
  # `moved` that card would have counted, in the two exit arms that read `moved` (below the summary
  # line). Placing it any earlier makes it answer for cards the other checks would have stopped,
  # and every such card then needs its old fate recomputed for the exit arms to stay where they
  # were. BEFORE the --dry-run branch, so a preview shows the withhold the real run would make and
  # does not count the parent as a would-move.
  if [ "$(printf '%s' "$row" | jq -r '.program')" = "true" ]; then
    echo "  ⊘ $ref (#$id): carries the \`$PROGRAM_TAG\` tag — this is a PARENT card naming SEVERAL legs, not one deliverable, so one release's ref may not take it to the terminal stage while its other legs are unbuilt. WITHHELD, not promoted, and left exactly where it was: move it by hand once every leg has shipped. The withhold does not change this run's exit code: the run exits as it would have if this card carried no tag and its move had landed." >&2
    program_held=$((program_held + 1)); continue
  fi
  if [ "$DRY" = 1 ]; then echo "  → $ref (#$id): would move $cur → $STAGE"; moved=$((moved + 1)); continue; fi
  if api -X PATCH -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "$(jq -nc --argjson s "$STAGE" '{workflow_stage_id: $s}')" "$API/tasks/$id.json" >/dev/null; then
    # ⛔ THE 2xx IS NOT THE MOVE (card#9938). The success line below is built out of a READ of
    # what the board now holds — the card#8556 contract, adopted verbatim: a mutation is
    # reported from a read-back, never from the status class. The read is issued ONCE and serves
    # the owner-tag clear too (see the read-back section above).
    read_rc=0
    back="$(api "$API/tasks/$id.json")" || read_rc=$?
    got="$(card_stage "$back")"
    case "$(stage_verdict "$read_rc" "$got" "$STAGE")" in
      applied)
        echo "  ✓ $ref (#$id): moved $cur → $STAGE (read back: the card is in stage $got)"
        moved=$((moved + 1))
        owner_clear "$id" "$ref" "$back"
        ;;
      not-applied)
        # NOT APPLIED AND KNOWN — rc 1's half of the contract, and it is a HARD FAILURE rather
        # than an unverified one because the read IS a measurement: the board answered, the card
        # was read, and it is somewhere else. This is the 2026-05-22 state (28/28 PATCHed,
        # `updated_at` bumped, the field never changed) said out loud.
        #
        # ⛔ ITS OWN COUNTER, NOT `failed`. `failed` is consumed by the "did this run promote
        # NOTHING AT ALL?" arm below, whose `moved == 0 && skipped == 0` terms are deliberate
        # FOR THAT QUESTION — so folding this outcome in gave it that arm's condition, and any
        # run that also promoted a card, or found one already released, printed this line and
        # exited 0. The counter is separate so the outcome can be gated on its own,
        # unconditionally, the way `unverified` already is.
        echo "  ✗ $ref (#$id): move NOT APPLIED — the PATCH answered success and the card reads back in stage $got, not $STAGE. The status is not the move; the read-back is." >&2
        not_applied=$((not_applied + 1))
        ;;
      *)
        # SENT, AND UNREADABLE. The card may be in either stage; this run says NEITHER. The
        # owner-tag clear is not attempted: its own write would be a second unverifiable one on a
        # card whose state is unknown, and its `tags` PATCH is authorized as an UPDATE, which
        # would be a worse thing to leave unknown than a tag nobody is acting on.
        if [ "$read_rc" -ne 0 ]; then why="$(resp_detail)"
        else why="the re-read answered success and no workflow_stage_id could be read out of it"; fi
        echo "  ⚠ $ref (#$id): move UNVERIFIED — the stage PATCH was SENT and answered success, but its outcome could NOT be read back ($why). Nothing here says the card moved and nothing says it did not; re-read before acting." >&2
        unverified=$((unverified + 1))
        ;;
    esac
  else
    move_rc=$?
    # ⛔ TWO OUTCOMES, TWO CLAIMS — and `(left in place)` is only true of ONE of them. A completed
    # non-2xx is a REFUSAL: the server read the PATCH, applied nothing, and the card really is
    # where it was. A transport failure is not: curl's connection can die AFTER the server applied
    # the write, so the card may well have MOVED — and the old line asserted `(left in place)` on
    # both, i.e. it stated as fact the very thing resp_detail's own sentence goes on to call
    # UNKNOWN. An operator reading "left in place" re-runs or hand-moves the card; on that branch
    # the tool had no basis for telling them to. The rc is api()'s own contract ($API_RC_REFUSED
    # for the answered-and-refused state, curl's verbatim rc otherwise), not a re-read of the
    # response — $API_ERR_FILE has exactly one reader by design (resp_detail).
    if [ "$move_rc" = "$API_RC_REFUSED" ]; then
      echo "  ✗ $ref (#$id): move failed (left in place) — $(resp_detail)" >&2
    else
      echo "  ✗ $ref (#$id): move NOT CONFIRMED — $(resp_detail)" >&2
    fi
    # ⚠ A NAMED RESIDUAL, not an oversight (card#9938). By the contract this file adopts, the
    # SECOND arm above — a request that never completed — is an UNVERIFIED write too: it was sent
    # and its outcome cannot be read. It is still counted as `failed`, i.e. rc 1's territory,
    # because card#9938 opened the un-applied-2xx axis only; moving a visibly-failed request from
    # rc 1 to rc 3 changes what this tool reports on an axis the card calls already working, and
    # that is its own decision to ask for. The LINE is honest either way — it says NOT CONFIRMED
    # and refuses to claim the card was left in place — so what is deferred is the exit code, not
    # the report.
    failed=$((failed + 1))
  fi
done <<< "$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -c '.[]')"

nmiss="$(printf '%s' "$MISSING" | jq 'length')"
if [ "${nmiss:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "promote-released-cards: WARNING — $nmiss shipped ref(s) matched NO card (unstamped card, or none): $(printf '%s' "$MISSING" | jq -r 'join(", ")')" >&2
fi
# Line 2 of the two-line no-card report (see the contract above). Its leading phrase is what
# release-pr-body greps, and it deliberately does NOT contain "matched NO card" — sharing that
# phrase would put these refs back on the line whose remedy is the wrong one. ⛔ NO COLON before
# the ref list: that reader cuts at the FIRST colon of the matched text.
nstranded="$(printf '%s' "$STRANDED_REFS" | jq 'length')"
if [ "${nstranded:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "promote-released-cards: WARNING — $nstranded shipped ref(s) matched ONLY an unsourced card, counting no card of THIS repo (the card exists and carries the ref, but has no by-ref source; a ref that ALSO matched another repo card is reported here rather than on the other-repo line, because this is the actionable half): $(printf '%s' "$STRANDED_REFS" | jq -r 'join(", ")')" >&2
fi
# The stage-guarded and repo-qualification counts are surfaced only when their guard is
# ACTIVE, so a run with no --shipped-stages on a `source: "*"` board keeps a byte-identical
# summary line (backward compatibility — a v0.21.0 fleet-adoption decision, asserted below by
# tests/promote-stage-guard-selftest.sh).
#
# THE UNVERIFIED AND NOT-APPLIED COUNTS RIDE BY THE SAME RULE, and for the same reason
# (card#9938): each appears only when there IS one, so every run that has always been possible
# keeps a byte-identical summary line, and a run that grew one of the new outcomes says so on
# the line an operator already reads. Neither is folded into `failed`: a failed move is a claim
# about a request the server refused, an unverified one is the refusal to make either claim, and
# a NOT-APPLIED one is a claim about what the BOARD HOLDS — three different things, and a
# summary that added them would report a run as a kind of failure it is not.
# A plain `if`, never `[ … ] && X=…`: under `set -e` that form EXITS the script on the false
# branch (and it is the same trailing-test spelling card#5874 records leaking a false status out
# of a sibling release bin).
#
# ⚠ `completeness-refused` RIDES BY A DIFFERENT RULE, and the paragraph above is not true of it:
# it is gated on the FLAG, not on its count, so `--require-complete` prints `0 completeness-
# refused` and that zero is deliberate (a gate asked about the wrong release head refuses
# nothing — the header's `--require-complete` § says to read the gate's state line before
# trusting it). Byte-identity is preserved by the same argument nonetheless, because the flag is
# opt-in: with `--require-complete` absent the segment is absent, so every run that was possible
# before card#10176 keeps the line it had. The two rules coexist on one line rather than being
# unified, because they answer different questions — "did this run grow a new outcome?" for the
# read-back counts, "was this run gated at all?" for the completeness one.
#
# SEGMENT ORDER is by WHEN a card leaves the promotion path, not by which card added the term:
# the pre-write refusals (`stage-guarded`, `completeness-refused`, `program-withheld`,
# `other-repo`/`unsourced`, `no-card`) come first in the order the move loop applies them, then
# the post-write outcomes (`NOT APPLIED`, `UNVERIFIED`, `failed`). `completeness-refused` sits
# immediately after `stage-guarded` because that is where its `continue` is in the loop, and
# `program-withheld` immediately after both because its `continue` is the last before the write.
#
# ⚠ `program-withheld` RIDES BY THE COUNT RULE, not the flag rule, because it has no flag: it
# appears only when there IS one, so every run that matches no `program`-tagged card keeps a
# byte-identical summary line and a run that withheld a parent says so on the line an operator
# already reads. ⚠ A run that DOES match one changes more than the new segment — the parent leaves
# `moved`, where the pre-change tool counted it — which is the honesty below, not a regression. It
# is its OWN segment and folded into NO other count, which is the honesty this whole guard turns on
# — a withheld card is neither promoted nor failed, and adding it to `moved` would make the one
# number a release step is read for assert the opposite of what happened.
unv_txt=""; na_txt=""; prog_txt=""
if [ "$not_applied" -gt 0 ]; then na_txt="$not_applied NOT APPLIED, "; fi
if [ "$unverified" -gt 0 ]; then unv_txt="$unverified UNVERIFIED, "; fi
if [ "$program_held" -gt 0 ]; then prog_txt="$program_held program-withheld, "; fi
echo "promote-released-cards: $moved moved, $skipped already-released, ${SHIPPED_STAGES:+$guarded stage-guarded, }$([ "$REQUIRE_COMPLETE" = 1 ] && printf '%s completeness-refused, ' "$incomplete")$prog_txt${QUALIFY_SRC:+$foreign_n other-repo, $unsourced_n unsourced, }$nmiss no-card, $na_txt$unv_txt$failed failed$([ "$DRY" = 1 ] && printf ' (dry-run)')."
# The guard's state, ALWAYS, on its own stderr line. #5144 made an explicitly-EMPTY
# --shipped-stages die, but the ABSENT case still printed a summary indistinguishable from
# a guarded run — so an unguarded promote read as clean, which is the same "looks enabled,
# isn't" shape the guard exists to prevent, one layer out.
#
# Deliberately NOT folded into the summary line above: that line is byte-identical when the
# guard is absent by an explicit v0.21.0 fleet-adoption decision, anything parsing it would
# break, and a selftest asserts it. Reporting on a SEPARATE stderr line gets the visibility
# without overturning that decision — the summary stays a stable parse target, and stderr is
# where this tool already puts its operator-facing findings (the no-card WARNING above, the
# per-card guard skips).
#
# THE PROGRAM WITHHOLD'S RUN-LEVEL LINE prints only when it WITHHELD something, and that is the
# opposite choice from the stage-guard pair directly below — so the difference is stated rather
# than left to look like an oversight. That pair exists because `--shipped-stages` can be ON or
# OFF and a run must say which, or an unguarded promote reads as a clean one. This withhold has no
# OFF: it is on for every run, on every install, so a line announcing it would tell every operator
# something true of every run that has ever been possible, and would change the stderr of all of
# them to say nothing. What an operator needs is the CARD, and that is what this prints.
# ⚠ Read `0 program-withheld`'s ABSENCE from the summary as "this run withheld no parent", never
# as "no parent was promoted": an untagged parent is invisible to the predicate (header).
if [ "$program_held" -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "promote-released-cards: $program_held card(s) carrying the \`$PROGRAM_TAG\` tag were WITHHELD from the terminal stage and are named individually above. They are PARENT cards naming several legs each; this release shipped one leg's ref, which does not finish them. Nothing was written for them and they are where they were — move each by hand once its every leg has shipped. This is not a failure: the withhold never raises this run's exit code, and changes it at all only where the move it did not make would itself have failed." >&2
fi
if [ -n "$SHIPPED_STAGES" ]; then
  echo "promote-released-cards: stage-guard ON (Shipped-class source stages: $SHIPPED_STAGES) — a matched card outside them is skipped, never promoted." >&2
else
  # ⚠ THE SCOPE WORD IS LOAD-BEARING AND IT IS NARROWER THAN IT WAS. This line used to say EVERY
  # DL/PR-matched card was promoted regardless of its stage, which stopped being true the day the
  # program-parent withhold landed: a matched parent is held back on a run with no --shipped-stages
  # at all, so the unqualified claim would have told an operator this run moved a card it did not.
  # A guard's stated scope has to equal its actual predicate, in both directions.
  echo "promote-released-cards: stage-guard OFF (no --shipped-stages) — every DL/PR-matched card this run promoted was promoted regardless of its current stage, so a stale or recycled stamp on a declined/backlog card would have moved it. Pass --shipped-stages <ids> to guard." >&2
fi
# The repo-qualification state, ALWAYS, on its own stderr line — for exactly the reason the
# stage-guard pair above exists (card#5152). `source: "*"` is a DECLARATION about the board,
# made in a config file nobody re-reads, and its whole effect is to switch this guard off; a
# run that acts on it must say so, or the declaration is unauditable after the fact. Separate
# line, not folded into the summary, so the summary stays the stable parse target it is.
if [ -n "$QUALIFY_SRC" ]; then
  echo "promote-released-cards: repo-qualification ON (source '$QUALIFY_SRC', from $SOURCE_SRC) — a ref-matched card belonging to another repo, or to no derivable repo, was NOT promoted." >&2
else
  echo "promote-released-cards: repo-qualification OFF — source '*' (from $SOURCE_SRC) DECLARES this board tracks exactly one repo, so a bare dl_number/pr_number is unambiguous on it. If it tracks several, set .promote.source to '<owner>/<repo>': those counters are repo-scoped and an unqualified match promotes another repo's cards." >&2
fi
# The completeness gate's state, only when it is ON: an OFF line would change the stderr of every
# run that never asked for the gate, and absent is byte-identical by the flag's own contract.
if [ "$REQUIRE_COMPLETE" = 1 ]; then
  echo "promote-released-cards: completeness-gate ON (oracle $COMPLETENESS; release head ${gate_head:-<UNRESOLVED>} from $gate_head_src; integration branch $gate_integration from $gate_integration_src) — a matched card that a PR outside this release still names, or whose PRs could not be read, was NOT promoted. THOSE TWO VALUES ARE THE POPULATION: the gate looked for PRs merged into that branch and absent from that commit, so a wrong pair reports 0 refusals over a window that was never the release's." >&2
  if [ "$incomplete" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "promote-released-cards: FAILED — $incomplete card(s) were REFUSED by the completeness gate (named individually above). Land or close the open PR, or release the merged one, and re-run — or, having checked, promote the card by hand." >&2
  fi
fi
# --- the run-level lines for the two READ-BACK outcomes, printed BEFORE any exit -------------
# ⛔ BOTH, WHATEVER THE RC. A run can carry a measured-wrong card AND an unreadable one, and the
# two ask for different things — "this card is in the wrong stage" and "go re-read these cards".
# The exit status can only say one of them, so neither line may sit behind the other's `exit`:
# printing them here, and deciding the rc below, is what keeps the losing outcome from going
# silent on the one run where both are true. Each prints only when its count is non-zero, so no
# run that was possible before card#9938 gains a line.
if [ "$not_applied" -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "promote-released-cards: $not_applied stage PATCH(es) answered success and the card(s) READ BACK IN ANOTHER STAGE — NOT APPLIED (rc 1). The board was read and it disagrees with the write: those cards are named above and are NOT released. Promote them by hand, or re-run this tool once the cause is fixed." >&2
fi
if [ "$unverified" -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "promote-released-cards: $unverified stage PATCH(es) were SENT and answered success, and their outcome could NOT be read back — UNVERIFIED WRITE (rc $RC_UNVERIFIED). Nothing here says those cards moved and nothing says they did not; re-read the cards named above before acting either way (re-running this tool is safe: a card already at the target stage is skipped as already-released)." >&2
fi

# --- exit policy: a "0 promoted" run is told apart by its ROOT CAUSE ----------
# A MEASURED un-applied write → exit 1, AND IT IS DECIDED FIRST (card#9938 round 2).
#
# ⛔ IT CARRIES NO RUN-SHAPE TERM, and that is the whole of this clause. The outcome was folded
# into `failed` when it shipped, which handed it the arm below's `moved == 0 && skipped == 0`
# condition — written for a different question — so a run that promoted ANY card, or found one
# already released, printed `✗ … move NOT APPLIED` on stderr and exited 0, green release step
# and all (measured: a two-card run, one moved and one un-applied, exits 0 on the pre-fix bin).
# The 2026-05-22 release was 28 for 28 un-applied, which that condition happens to catch — one
# card landing is all it takes to lose it, and a release promotes many cards. A card the board
# has been READ and SAID is in the wrong stage is a failed promotion whatever else in the run
# succeeded; the counts say what else happened, and the rc says the release is not clean.
#
# ⛔ IT OUTRANKS $RC_UNVERIFIED, deliberately, and the asymmetry is the argument: rc 3 exists to
# say NOBODY KNOWS, and rc 1 is a MEASUREMENT that the write did not take. A run holding both
# knows one of its cards is in the wrong stage, and answering `3` — documented as "not always a
# failed one" — would hedge a question this run has already answered. The UNVERIFIED line is
# still printed above, so nothing is lost but the rc, which can only carry one verdict.
#
# ⛔ AND IT IS DECIDED BEFORE THE COMPLETENESS DIE BELOW, for the reason the unverified arm
# gives: that die's premise (the refs never got here) is FALSE on this run — they got here,
# matched cards, and were PATCHed. rc 2 would send the operator at the wrong question.
if [ "$not_applied" -gt 0 ]; then exit 1; fi
# Systemic move failure (a card matched but every PATCH errored) → exit 1.
#
# ⛔ `$unverified` IS DELIBERATELY NOT A TERM HERE, and the card#8556 contract is what keeps it
# out rather than what puts it in. That contract rules on ONE write: rc 1 is NOT APPLIED **AND
# KNOWN**, so the write this rc speaks for must be one somebody measured — and a REFUSED move is
# measured (the server answered, and this file's own line for it says the card is `left in
# place`). `$unverified` counts OTHER cards, the ones nobody could rule on, and says nothing
# about the refused one. A term for it here would let an unreadable card demote a measured
# refusal to rc 3 — "not always a failed one / re-running is safe", advice that is wrong for a
# 403 — which is precisely what the EXIT CODES block's unscoped `1 OUTRANKS 3` rule forbids. The
# term was carried here for one round of card#9938 and never shipped; the run shape it decided
# (a refused move beside an unreadable read-back) is now driven in
# `tests/promote-move-readback-selftest.sh` § 6, which reds if it comes back.
#
# ⚠ THIS ARM ANSWERS ONE QUESTION — "did this run promote NOTHING AT ALL?" — and its `moved == 0
# && skipped == 0 && program_held == 0` terms are that question's, not a general failure
# policy. `$failed` now holds ONLY the visibly-failed request (a refused PATCH, and the
# never-completed one recorded as a residual at the move loop): card#9938's measured-wrong-stage
# outcome has its own counter and
# its own unconditional clause ABOVE, because giving a new outcome an old counter gave it that
# counter's run-shape condition too. This clause is the one card#9938 found here plus ONE term,
# `program_held == 0` (card#10068). The withhold is the LAST check before the write, so every card
# `program_held` counts passed every other check and is one the pre-withhold bin PATCHed and, once
# the PATCH landed, counted `moved` — which kept this arm quiet. So the term changes no rc a run
# whose parent's move would have landed ever had; where that move would have been refused (the one
# case nothing here can know without sending it) the pre-withhold run was rc 1 only if nothing else
# moved, and this run is rc 0 — a lowering, never a raise. A parent the stage guard or the
# completeness gate stops never reaches the withhold, is counted `guarded` / `incomplete` as it
# always was, and leaves this arm exactly as armed as before (`tests/promote-program-withhold-
# selftest.sh` § 9). Without the term, a parent beside a refused move turned a run that has always
# exited 0 into rc 1 (§ 6). It is its own term, not a fold into `moved`, for the reason the summary
# line gives: a withheld card was not promoted.
#
# ⚠ A NAMED RESIDUAL, on the SAME axis and deliberately not closed here: a VISIBLY refused or
# never-completed move beside a card that DID move (or an already-released one, or a withheld
# parent) still exits 0 — `moved == 0 && skipped == 0 && program_held == 0` is why. That is the
# acceptance card#9301 shipped, on a run shape that predates card#9938; widening it changes
# what this tool reports on runs it has always reported as green, which is its own decision to ask
# for and is not this card's. What card#9938 owes is that the DOCS say so, and they do in ONE place:
# the header's EXIT CODES block, which `--help` prints and which states the condition rather than
# the unconditional rule that was true
# of neither half. Every other surface points there rather than carrying a copy — that block
# publishes the command that re-derives which surfaces those are.
if [ "$failed" -gt 0 ] && [ "$moved" = 0 ] && [ "$skipped" = 0 ] && [ "$program_held" = 0 ]; then exit 1; fi
# A WRITE WENT OUT AND NOBODY HERE CAN SAY WHAT IT DID → exit $RC_UNVERIFIED (card#9938).
#
# ⛔ IT IS DECIDED BEFORE THE COMPLETENESS DIE BELOW, because that die's premise is FALSE on this
# run. It exists to catch a squash-merged release whose per-PR refs were dropped — "0 promoted"
# read as "the refs never got here". Here the refs got here, matched cards, and were PATCHed; the
# only thing missing is the read. Telling an operator to go looking for dropped refs would send
# them at the wrong question, at an exit code (2) that means bad invocation.
#
# ⚠ AND IT FIRES WITH `moved` > 0 TOO. A release in which 27 cards read back moved and one did
# not read back at all is not a clean release: the record is incomplete, and rc 0 is this tool
# saying it is not. That is the whole of what card#9938 changes for a run that used to be green.
# Its LINE is printed above rather than here, so a run that also carries a measured-wrong card
# (which exits 1 first) still tells the operator which cards to go and re-read.
#
# ⚠ A NAMED RESIDUAL — THE `failed` PAIRING IS DRIVEN BY NO LEG, and this note is the record of
# that rather than a row (card#9938). This clause reads NO run-shape counter, so the four
# pairings a term could be added for are not equally covered: `moved` is driven by 6c, `skipped`
# by 6j, `not_applied` is INERT — its own clause above exits 1 first, so this line is unreachable
# with `not_applied` > 0 and a term for it could never change an rc — and `failed` is driven by
# NOTHING. That last cell is live: the rc-1 clause above requires `moved` = 0 AND `skipped` = 0,
# so a VISIBLY refused move beside a card that did move does not exit 1, and this clause is what
# answers. MEASURED: adding `&& [ "$failed" = 0 ]` here drops such a run from rc 3 to rc 0 with a
# byte-identical summary, and that mutant passes all seven promote suites plus the parity
# selftest. The gap is the same shape 6i names for the `not_applied` clause, one clause over.
# ⛔ NOT CLOSED HERE, and deliberately: a row for it is new coverage on a reviewed branch, and the
# reader who needs this is the one about to ADD a term — which this says not to do, and says why.
if [ "$unverified" -gt 0 ]; then exit "$RC_UNVERIFIED"; fi
# Newly promoted NOTHING (moved==0) despite a non-empty shipped-ref set (an empty set
# already exited "nothing to do"). Two distinct causes, told apart by whether ref-
# derivation was COMPLETE:
#   - complete (an explicit --dls/--cards set, or a MERGE-commit release tip whose range carries every
#     feature/prep subject) → the derived DLs are genuinely cardless (docs-only, or tracked
#     elsewhere) → the WARNING above suffices, exit 0. An idempotent re-run of a legit
#     merge-commit release (everything already at target, skipped>0) lands here too.
#   - INCOMPLETE (a non-merge tip → the release PR was squash/rebase-merged, collapsing the
#     per-PR subjects this reads) → cards exist but went unmatched (the v0.32.0 silent-green
#     incident) → FAIL loud so the operator verifies + promotes by hand.
# NOTE: `skipped` is deliberately NOT in the guard — a squash release whose siblings were
# already promoted (a partial/earlier run) still has moved==0 and must FAIL; the legit
# merge-commit re-run is exempted by the tip check, not by skipped. `guarded` is NOT in
# the guard either: a source-stage-skipped card proves only that a DL token survived the
# squash — the die guards PR-marker completeness, and the squashed feature commits' (#NNN)
# markers can be lost while a title DL still matches. A guarded-skip therefore cannot
# disprove a dropped sibling ref; on this off-process path (non-merge tip only) the
# ~2-minute manual verification a false alarm costs is the right side of the asymmetry
# vs a silently un-promoted shipped card. `program_held` IS in the guard, and that is not the
# same call made the other way — it is a different constraint winning (card#10068). A withheld
# parent proves a ref reached a card, not that the RANGE was complete, so on the die's own
# question it is no better evidence than a guarded skip. But the withhold is the last check before
# the write, so every card it counts is one the pre-withhold bin PATCHed and counted `moved`, which
# kept this die from firing; leaving it out made a release whose only match was a parent go from
# rc 0 to rc 2 — a withheld card breaking a consumer's release, the one thing THE PROGRAM-PARENT
# WITHHOLD (header) is shaped not to do. A parent the stage guard or the completeness gate stops
# never reaches the withhold, is not in `program_held`, and the die fires on it exactly as before
# (selftest § 8, and § 10's squash-tip cell). Dry-run counted a would-move as `moved` too, so the
# preview agrees. The residual this leaves is the one that run always had: a squash release whose
# ONLY surviving ref was a parent's is not told apart from one whose other refs were dropped. The
# withhold still names the parent on stderr, so the operator reading that run is pointed at the
# card this run could see.
# The card#8421 counts (`foreign`, `unsourced`) are
# NOT in the guard either, and for the same reason: a repo-qualification rejection says
# nothing about whether the RANGE was complete, which is the only question this die asks. A
# release whose refs ALL matched other repos therefore still fails behind a squash tip — which
# is right, because the two possible causes (nothing of ours shipped / our refs were dropped)
# are exactly what a human has to tell apart there.
if [ "$moved" = 0 ] && [ "$program_held" = 0 ] && [ -z "$DLS_IN$CARDS_IN" ] && ! merge_commit_tip "$HEAD_REF"; then
  die "FAILED — 0 cards newly promoted and the release tip (${HEAD_REF}) is not a merge commit. A squash/rebase merge of the release PR may have dropped the per-PR refs this derives from (use a MERGE commit for release/sync PRs — VERSIONING.md). Verify and promote manually."
fi
# A COMPLETENESS REFUSAL IS LAST ON THE LADDER, and non-zero rather than a warning: a card left
# in its shipped stage is recoverable on the next release, a card wrongly in the terminal stage
# is one nobody re-reads. It ranks BELOW EVERY cause above on purpose — those mean "this run
# is degraded", this one means "this run did the right thing and a human must finish or close
# something" — so it never masks them. Only reachable under --require-complete.
if [ "$incomplete" -gt 0 ]; then exit 5; fi
exit 0
